window.SHAKESPEARE_ENGLISH = window.SHAKESPEARE_ENGLISH || {};
window.SHAKESPEARE_ENGLISH["cleopatra"] = {"id":"cleopatra","title":"Antony and Cleopatra","kind":"play","source":"MIT Shakespeare (Complete Moby Shakespeare)","sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","sourceRevision":"6b82db852c7322dd33e95db347f0ddfc812c6409","sections":[{"id":"1.1","label":"Act 1 · SCENE I. Alexandria. A room in CLEOPATRA's palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DEMETRIUS and PHILO","id":"cleopatra:1.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, but this dotage of our general's\nO'erflows the measure: those his goodly eyes,\nThat o'er the files and musters of the war\nHave glow'd like plated Mars, now bend, now turn,\nThe office and devotion of their view\nUpon a tawny front: his captain's heart,\nWhich in the scuffles of great fights hath burst\nThe buckles on his breast, reneges all temper,\nAnd is become the bellows and the fan\nTo cool a gipsy's lust.","sourceLine":"1.1.1","speaker":"PHILO","id":"cleopatra:1.1:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter ANTONY, CLEOPATRA, her Ladies, the Train, with Eunuchs fanning her","id":"cleopatra:1.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look, where they come:\nTake but good note, and you shall see in him.\nThe triple pillar of the world transform'd\nInto a strumpet's fool: behold and see.","sourceLine":"1.1.11","speaker":"PHILO","id":"cleopatra:1.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If it be love indeed, tell me how much.","sourceLine":"1.1.15","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's beggary in the love that can be reckon'd.","sourceLine":"1.1.16","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll set a bourn how far to be beloved.","sourceLine":"1.1.17","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then must thou needs find out new heaven, new earth.","sourceLine":"1.1.18","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter an Attendant","id":"cleopatra:1.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"News, my good lord, from Rome.","sourceLine":"1.1.19","speaker":"Attendant","id":"cleopatra:1.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Grates me: the sum.","sourceLine":"1.1.20","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, hear them, Antony:\nFulvia perchance is angry; or, who knows\nIf the scarce-bearded Caesar have not sent\nHis powerful mandate to you, 'Do this, or this;\nTake in that kingdom, and enfranchise that;\nPerform 't, or else we damn thee.'","sourceLine":"1.1.21","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How, my love!","sourceLine":"1.1.27","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Perchance! nay, and most like:\nYou must not stay here longer, your dismission\nIs come from Caesar; therefore hear it, Antony.\nWhere's Fulvia's process? Caesar's I would say? both?\nCall in the messengers. As I am Egypt's queen,\nThou blushest, Antony; and that blood of thine\nIs Caesar's homager: else so thy cheek pays shame\nWhen shrill-tongued Fulvia scolds. The messengers!","sourceLine":"1.1.28","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let Rome in Tiber melt, and the wide arch\nOf the ranged empire fall! Here is my space.\nKingdoms are clay: our dungy earth alike\nFeeds beast as man: the nobleness of life\nIs to do thus; when such a mutual pair","sourceLine":"1.1.36","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Embracing","id":"cleopatra:1.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And such a twain can do't, in which I bind,\nOn pain of punishment, the world to weet\nWe stand up peerless.","sourceLine":"1.1.41","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Excellent falsehood!\nWhy did he marry Fulvia, and not love her?\nI'll seem the fool I am not; Antony\nWill be himself.","sourceLine":"1.1.44","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But stirr'd by Cleopatra.\nNow, for the love of Love and her soft hours,\nLet's not confound the time with conference harsh:\nThere's not a minute of our lives should stretch\nWithout some pleasure now. What sport tonight?","sourceLine":"1.1.48","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear the ambassadors.","sourceLine":"1.1.53","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fie, wrangling queen!\nWhom every thing becomes, to chide, to laugh,\nTo weep; whose every passion fully strives\nTo make itself, in thee, fair and admired!\nNo messenger, but thine; and all alone\nTo-night we'll wander through the streets and note\nThe qualities of people. Come, my queen;\nLast night you did desire it: speak not to us.","sourceLine":"1.1.54","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.1:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt MARK ANTONY and CLEOPATRA with their train","id":"cleopatra:1.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is Caesar with Antonius prized so slight?","sourceLine":"1.1.62","speaker":"DEMETRIUS","id":"cleopatra:1.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, sometimes, when he is not Antony,\nHe comes too short of that great property\nWhich still should go with Antony.","sourceLine":"1.1.63","speaker":"PHILO","id":"cleopatra:1.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am full sorry\nThat he approves the common liar, who\nThus speaks of him at Rome: but I will hope\nOf better deeds to-morrow. Rest you happy!","sourceLine":"1.1.66","speaker":"DEMETRIUS","id":"cleopatra:1.1:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:1.1:26"}],"scene":1},{"id":"1.2","label":"Act 1 · SCENE II. The same. Another room.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CHARMIAN, IRAS, ALEXAS, and a Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord Alexas, sweet Alexas, most any thing Alexas,\nalmost most absolute Alexas, where's the soothsayer\nthat you praised so to the queen? O, that I knew\nthis husband, which, you say, must charge his horns\nwith garlands!","sourceLine":"1.2.1","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Soothsayer!","sourceLine":"1.2.6","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your will?","sourceLine":"1.2.7","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is this the man? Is't you, sir, that know things?","sourceLine":"1.2.8","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In nature's infinite book of secrecy\nA little I can read.","sourceLine":"1.2.9","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Show him your hand.","sourceLine":"1.2.11","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bring in the banquet quickly; wine enough\nCleopatra's health to drink.","sourceLine":"1.2.12","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good sir, give me good fortune.","sourceLine":"1.2.14","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I make not, but foresee.","sourceLine":"1.2.15","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, then, foresee me one.","sourceLine":"1.2.16","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall be yet far fairer than you are.","sourceLine":"1.2.17","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He means in flesh.","sourceLine":"1.2.18","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, you shall paint when you are old.","sourceLine":"1.2.19","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wrinkles forbid!","sourceLine":"1.2.20","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Vex not his prescience; be attentive.","sourceLine":"1.2.21","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hush!","sourceLine":"1.2.22","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall be more beloving than beloved.","sourceLine":"1.2.23","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I had rather heat my liver with drinking.","sourceLine":"1.2.24","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, hear him.","sourceLine":"1.2.25","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good now, some excellent fortune! Let me be married\nto three kings in a forenoon, and widow them all:\nlet me have a child at fifty, to whom Herod of Jewry\nmay do homage: find me to marry me with Octavius\nCaesar, and companion me with my mistress.","sourceLine":"1.2.26","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall outlive the lady whom you serve.","sourceLine":"1.2.31","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O excellent! I love long life better than figs.","sourceLine":"1.2.32","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have seen and proved a fairer former fortune\nThan that which is to approach.","sourceLine":"1.2.33","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then belike my children shall have no names:\nprithee, how many boys and wenches must I have?","sourceLine":"1.2.35","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If every of your wishes had a womb.\nAnd fertile every wish, a million.","sourceLine":"1.2.37","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Out, fool! I forgive thee for a witch.","sourceLine":"1.2.39","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You think none but your sheets are privy to your wishes.","sourceLine":"1.2.40","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, come, tell Iras hers.","sourceLine":"1.2.41","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll know all our fortunes.","sourceLine":"1.2.42","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mine, and most of our fortunes, to-night, shall\nbe--drunk to bed.","sourceLine":"1.2.43","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's a palm presages chastity, if nothing else.","sourceLine":"1.2.45","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"E'en as the o'erflowing Nilus presageth famine.","sourceLine":"1.2.46","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, you wild bedfellow, you cannot soothsay.","sourceLine":"1.2.47","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, if an oily palm be not a fruitful\nprognostication, I cannot scratch mine ear. Prithee,\ntell her but a worky-day fortune.","sourceLine":"1.2.48","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your fortunes are alike.","sourceLine":"1.2.51","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But how, but how? give me particulars.","sourceLine":"1.2.52","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have said.","sourceLine":"1.2.53","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:1.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Am I not an inch of fortune better than she?","sourceLine":"1.2.54","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, if you were but an inch of fortune better than\nI, where would you choose it?","sourceLine":"1.2.55","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not in my husband's nose.","sourceLine":"1.2.57","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our worser thoughts heavens mend! Alexas,--come,\nhis fortune, his fortune! O, let him marry a woman\nthat cannot go, sweet Isis, I beseech thee! and let\nher die too, and give him a worse! and let worst\nfollow worse, till the worst of all follow him\nlaughing to his grave, fifty-fold a cuckold! Good\nIsis, hear me this prayer, though thou deny me a\nmatter of more weight; good Isis, I beseech thee!","sourceLine":"1.2.58","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Amen. Dear goddess, hear that prayer of the people!\nfor, as it is a heartbreaking to see a handsome man\nloose-wived, so it is a deadly sorrow to behold a\nfoul knave uncuckolded: therefore, dear Isis, keep\ndecorum, and fortune him accordingly!","sourceLine":"1.2.66","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Amen.","sourceLine":"1.2.71","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lo, now, if it lay in their hands to make me a\ncuckold, they would make themselves whores, but\nthey'ld do't!","sourceLine":"1.2.72","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hush! here comes Antony.","sourceLine":"1.2.75","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not he; the queen.","sourceLine":"1.2.76","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Saw you my lord?","sourceLine":"1.2.77","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, lady.","sourceLine":"1.2.78","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was he not here?","sourceLine":"1.2.79","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, madam.","sourceLine":"1.2.80","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He was disposed to mirth; but on the sudden\nA Roman thought hath struck him. Enobarbus!","sourceLine":"1.2.81","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam?","sourceLine":"1.2.83","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Seek him, and bring him hither.\nWhere's Alexas?","sourceLine":"1.2.84","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, at your service. My lord approaches.","sourceLine":"1.2.86","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We will not look upon him: go with us.","sourceLine":"1.2.87","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.2:58"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:1.2:59"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY with a Messenger and Attendants","id":"cleopatra:1.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fulvia thy wife first came into the field.","sourceLine":"1.2.88","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Against my brother Lucius?","sourceLine":"1.2.89","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay:\nBut soon that war had end, and the time's state\nMade friends of them, joining their force 'gainst Caesar;\nWhose better issue in the war, from Italy,\nUpon the first encounter, drave them.","sourceLine":"1.2.90","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, what worst?","sourceLine":"1.2.95","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The nature of bad news infects the teller.","sourceLine":"1.2.96","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When it concerns the fool or coward. On:\nThings that are past are done with me. 'Tis thus:\nWho tells me true, though in his tale lie death,\nI hear him as he flatter'd.","sourceLine":"1.2.97","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Labienus--\nThis is stiff news--hath, with his Parthian force,\nExtended Asia from Euphrates;\nHis conquering banner shook from Syria\nTo Lydia and to Ionia; Whilst--","sourceLine":"1.2.101","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Antony, thou wouldst say,--","sourceLine":"1.2.106","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, my lord!","sourceLine":"1.2.107","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Speak to me home, mince not the general tongue:\nName Cleopatra as she is call'd in Rome;\nRail thou in Fulvia's phrase; and taunt my faults\nWith such full licence as both truth and malice\nHave power to utter. O, then we bring forth weeds,\nWhen our quick minds lie still; and our ills told us\nIs as our earing. Fare thee well awhile.","sourceLine":"1.2.108","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At your noble pleasure.","sourceLine":"1.2.115","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:1.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From Sicyon, ho, the news! Speak there!","sourceLine":"1.2.116","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The man from Sicyon,--is there such an one?","sourceLine":"1.2.117","speaker":"First Attendant","id":"cleopatra:1.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He stays upon your will.","sourceLine":"1.2.118","speaker":"Second Attendant","id":"cleopatra:1.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him appear.\nThese strong Egyptian fetters I must break,\nOr lose myself in dotage.","sourceLine":"1.2.119","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:76"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter another Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What are you?","sourceLine":"1.2.122","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fulvia thy wife is dead.","sourceLine":"1.2.123","speaker":"Second Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where died she?","sourceLine":"1.2.124","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In Sicyon:\nHer length of sickness, with what else more serious\nImporteth thee to know, this bears.","sourceLine":"1.2.125","speaker":"Second Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:81"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Gives a letter","id":"cleopatra:1.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Forbear me.","sourceLine":"1.2.128","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:83"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Second Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's a great spirit gone! Thus did I desire it:\nWhat our contempt doth often hurl from us,\nWe wish it ours again; the present pleasure,\nBy revolution lowering, does become\nThe opposite of itself: she's good, being gone;\nThe hand could pluck her back that shoved her on.\nI must from this enchanting queen break off:\nTen thousand harms, more than the ills I know,\nMy idleness doth hatch. How now! Enobarbus!","sourceLine":"1.2.129","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:85"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's your pleasure, sir?","sourceLine":"1.2.138","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must with haste from hence.","sourceLine":"1.2.139","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then, we kill all our women:\nwe see how mortal an unkindness is to them;\nif they suffer our departure, death's the word.","sourceLine":"1.2.140","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must be gone.","sourceLine":"1.2.143","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Under a compelling occasion, let women die; it were\npity to cast them away for nothing; though, between\nthem and a great cause, they should be esteemed\nnothing. Cleopatra, catching but the least noise of\nthis, dies instantly; I have seen her die twenty\ntimes upon far poorer moment: I do think there is\nmettle in death, which commits some loving act upon\nher, she hath such a celerity in dying.","sourceLine":"1.2.144","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is cunning past man's thought.","sourceLine":"1.2.152","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:92"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alack, sir, no; her passions are made of nothing but\nthe finest part of pure love: we cannot call her\nwinds and waters sighs and tears; they are greater\nstorms and tempests than almanacs can report: this\ncannot be cunning in her; if it be, she makes a\nshower of rain as well as Jove.","sourceLine":"1.2.153","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would I had never seen her.","sourceLine":"1.2.159","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, sir, you had then left unseen a wonderful piece\nof work; which not to have been blest withal would\nhave discredited your travel.","sourceLine":"1.2.160","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fulvia is dead.","sourceLine":"1.2.163","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir?","sourceLine":"1.2.164","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fulvia is dead.","sourceLine":"1.2.165","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fulvia!","sourceLine":"1.2.166","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dead.","sourceLine":"1.2.167","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, sir, give the gods a thankful sacrifice. When\nit pleaseth their deities to take the wife of a man\nfrom him, it shows to man the tailors of the earth;\ncomforting therein, that when old robes are worn\nout, there are members to make new. If there were\nno more women but Fulvia, then had you indeed a cut,\nand the case to be lamented: this grief is crowned\nwith consolation; your old smock brings forth a new\npetticoat: and indeed the tears live in an onion\nthat should water this sorrow.","sourceLine":"1.2.168","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The business she hath broached in the state\nCannot endure my absence.","sourceLine":"1.2.178","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And the business you have broached here cannot be\nwithout you; especially that of Cleopatra's, which\nwholly depends on your abode.","sourceLine":"1.2.180","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more light answers. Let our officers\nHave notice what we purpose. I shall break\nThe cause of our expedience to the queen,\nAnd get her leave to part. For not alone\nThe death of Fulvia, with more urgent touches,\nDo strongly speak to us; but the letters too\nOf many our contriving friends in Rome\nPetition us at home: Sextus Pompeius\nHath given the dare to Caesar, and commands\nThe empire of the sea: our slippery people,\nWhose love is never link'd to the deserver\nTill his deserts are past, begin to throw\nPompey the Great and all his dignities\nUpon his son; who, high in name and power,\nHigher than both in blood and life, stands up\nFor the main soldier: whose quality, going on,\nThe sides o' the world may danger: much is breeding,\nWhich, like the courser's hair, hath yet but life,\nAnd not a serpent's poison. Say, our pleasure,\nTo such whose place is under us, requires\nOur quick remove from hence.","sourceLine":"1.2.183","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.2:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall do't.","sourceLine":"1.2.204","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:1.2:106"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:1.2:107"}],"scene":2},{"id":"1.3","label":"Act 1 · SCENE III. The same. Another room.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, CHARMIAN, IRAS, and ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is he?","sourceLine":"1.3.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did not see him since.","sourceLine":"1.3.2","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See where he is, who's with him, what he does:\nI did not send you: if you find him sad,\nSay I am dancing; if in mirth, report\nThat I am sudden sick: quick, and return.","sourceLine":"1.3.3","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, methinks, if you did love him dearly,\nYou do not hold the method to enforce\nThe like from him.","sourceLine":"1.3.7","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What should I do, I do not?","sourceLine":"1.3.10","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In each thing give him way, cross him nothing.","sourceLine":"1.3.11","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou teachest like a fool; the way to lose him.","sourceLine":"1.3.12","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tempt him not so too far; I wish, forbear:\nIn time we hate that which we often fear.\nBut here comes Antony.","sourceLine":"1.3.13","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.3:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am sick and sullen.","sourceLine":"1.3.16","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am sorry to give breathing to my purpose,--","sourceLine":"1.3.17","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Help me away, dear Charmian; I shall fall:\nIt cannot be thus long, the sides of nature\nWill not sustain it.","sourceLine":"1.3.18","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, my dearest queen,--","sourceLine":"1.3.21","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray you, stand further from me.","sourceLine":"1.3.22","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's the matter?","sourceLine":"1.3.23","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know, by that same eye, there's some good news.\nWhat says the married woman? You may go:\nWould she had never given you leave to come!\nLet her not say 'tis I that keep you here:\nI have no power upon you; hers you are.","sourceLine":"1.3.24","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods best know,--","sourceLine":"1.3.29","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, never was there queen\nSo mightily betray'd! yet at the first\nI saw the treasons planted.","sourceLine":"1.3.30","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cleopatra,--","sourceLine":"1.3.33","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why should I think you can be mine and true,\nThough you in swearing shake the throned gods,\nWho have been false to Fulvia? Riotous madness,\nTo be entangled with those mouth-made vows,\nWhich break themselves in swearing!","sourceLine":"1.3.34","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most sweet queen,--","sourceLine":"1.3.39","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, pray you, seek no colour for your going,\nBut bid farewell, and go: when you sued staying,\nThen was the time for words: no going then;\nEternity was in our lips and eyes,\nBliss in our brows' bent; none our parts so poor,\nBut was a race of heaven: they are so still,\nOr thou, the greatest soldier of the world,\nArt turn'd the greatest liar.","sourceLine":"1.3.40","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, lady!","sourceLine":"1.3.48","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I had thy inches; thou shouldst know\nThere were a heart in Egypt.","sourceLine":"1.3.49","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear me, queen:\nThe strong necessity of time commands\nOur services awhile; but my full heart\nRemains in use with you. Our Italy\nShines o'er with civil swords: Sextus Pompeius\nMakes his approaches to the port of Rome:\nEquality of two domestic powers\nBreed scrupulous faction: the hated, grown to strength,\nAre newly grown to love: the condemn'd Pompey,\nRich in his father's honour, creeps apace,\nInto the hearts of such as have not thrived\nUpon the present state, whose numbers threaten;\nAnd quietness, grown sick of rest, would purge\nBy any desperate change: my more particular,\nAnd that which most with you should safe my going,\nIs Fulvia's death.","sourceLine":"1.3.51","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Though age from folly could not give me freedom,\nIt does from childishness: can Fulvia die?","sourceLine":"1.3.67","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She's dead, my queen:\nLook here, and at thy sovereign leisure read\nThe garboils she awaked; at the last, best:\nSee when and where she died.","sourceLine":"1.3.69","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O most false love!\nWhere be the sacred vials thou shouldst fill\nWith sorrowful water? Now I see, I see,\nIn Fulvia's death, how mine received shall be.","sourceLine":"1.3.73","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Quarrel no more, but be prepared to know\nThe purposes I bear; which are, or cease,\nAs you shall give the advice. By the fire\nThat quickens Nilus' slime, I go from hence\nThy soldier, servant; making peace or war\nAs thou affect'st.","sourceLine":"1.3.77","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cut my lace, Charmian, come;\nBut let it be: I am quickly ill, and well,\nSo Antony loves.","sourceLine":"1.3.83","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My precious queen, forbear;\nAnd give true evidence to his love, which stands\nAn honourable trial.","sourceLine":"1.3.86","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So Fulvia told me.\nI prithee, turn aside and weep for her,\nThen bid adieu to me, and say the tears\nBelong to Egypt: good now, play one scene\nOf excellent dissembling; and let it look\nLife perfect honour.","sourceLine":"1.3.89","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You'll heat my blood: no more.","sourceLine":"1.3.95","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You can do better yet; but this is meetly.","sourceLine":"1.3.96","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, by my sword,--","sourceLine":"1.3.97","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And target. Still he mends;\nBut this is not the best. Look, prithee, Charmian,\nHow this Herculean Roman does become\nThe carriage of his chafe.","sourceLine":"1.3.98","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll leave you, lady.","sourceLine":"1.3.102","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Courteous lord, one word.\nSir, you and I must part, but that's not it:\nSir, you and I have loved, but there's not it;\nThat you know well: something it is I would,\nO, my oblivion is a very Antony,\nAnd I am all forgotten.","sourceLine":"1.3.103","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But that your royalty\nHolds idleness your subject, I should take you\nFor idleness itself.","sourceLine":"1.3.109","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis sweating labour\nTo bear such idleness so near the heart\nAs Cleopatra this. But, sir, forgive me;\nSince my becomings kill me, when they do not\nEye well to you: your honour calls you hence;\nTherefore be deaf to my unpitied folly.\nAnd all the gods go with you! upon your sword\nSit laurel victory! and smooth success\nBe strew'd before your feet!","sourceLine":"1.3.112","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us go. Come;\nOur separation so abides, and flies,\nThat thou, residing here, go'st yet with me,\nAnd I, hence fleeting, here remain with thee. Away!","sourceLine":"1.3.121","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:1.3:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:1.3:44"}],"scene":3},{"id":"1.4","label":"Act 1 · SCENE IV. Rome. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's house.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, reading a letter, LEPIDUS, and their Train","id":"cleopatra:1.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You may see, Lepidus, and henceforth know,\nIt is not Caesar's natural vice to hate\nOur great competitor: from Alexandria\nThis is the news: he fishes, drinks, and wastes\nThe lamps of night in revel; is not more man-like\nThan Cleopatra; nor the queen of Ptolemy\nMore womanly than he; hardly gave audience, or\nVouchsafed to think he had partners: you shall find there\nA man who is the abstract of all faults\nThat all men follow.","sourceLine":"1.4.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must not think there are\nEvils enow to darken all his goodness:\nHis faults in him seem as the spots of heaven,\nMore fiery by night's blackness; hereditary,\nRather than purchased; what he cannot change,\nThan what he chooses.","sourceLine":"1.4.11","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:1.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are too indulgent. Let us grant, it is not\nAmiss to tumble on the bed of Ptolemy;\nTo give a kingdom for a mirth; to sit\nAnd keep the turn of tippling with a slave;\nTo reel the streets at noon, and stand the buffet\nWith knaves that smell of sweat: say this\nbecomes him,--\nAs his composure must be rare indeed\nWhom these things cannot blemish,--yet must Antony\nNo way excuse his soils, when we do bear\nSo great weight in his lightness. If he fill'd\nHis vacancy with his voluptuousness,\nFull surfeits, and the dryness of his bones,\nCall on him for't: but to confound such time,\nThat drums him from his sport, and speaks as loud\nAs his own state and ours,--'tis to be chid\nAs we rate boys, who, being mature in knowledge,\nPawn their experience to their present pleasure,\nAnd so rebel to judgment.","sourceLine":"1.4.17","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.4:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here's more news.","sourceLine":"1.4.36","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:1.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy biddings have been done; and every hour,\nMost noble Caesar, shalt thou have report\nHow 'tis abroad. Pompey is strong at sea;\nAnd it appears he is beloved of those\nThat only have fear'd Caesar: to the ports\nThe discontents repair, and men's reports\nGive him much wrong'd.","sourceLine":"1.4.37","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I should have known no less.\nIt hath been taught us from the primal state,\nThat he which is was wish'd until he were;\nAnd the ebb'd man, ne'er loved till ne'er worth love,\nComes dear'd by being lack'd. This common body,\nLike to a vagabond flag upon the stream,\nGoes to and back, lackeying the varying tide,\nTo rot itself with motion.","sourceLine":"1.4.44","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, I bring thee word,\nMenecrates and Menas, famous pirates,\nMake the sea serve them, which they ear and wound\nWith keels of every kind: many hot inroads\nThey make in Italy; the borders maritime\nLack blood to think on't, and flush youth revolt:\nNo vessel can peep forth, but 'tis as soon\nTaken as seen; for Pompey's name strikes more\nThan could his war resisted.","sourceLine":"1.4.52","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:1.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Antony,\nLeave thy lascivious wassails. When thou once\nWast beaten from Modena, where thou slew'st\nHirtius and Pansa, consuls, at thy heel\nDid famine follow; whom thou fought'st against,\nThough daintily brought up, with patience more\nThan savages could suffer: thou didst drink\nThe stale of horses, and the gilded puddle\nWhich beasts would cough at: thy palate then did deign\nThe roughest berry on the rudest hedge;\nYea, like the stag, when snow the pasture sheets,\nThe barks of trees thou browsed'st; on the Alps\nIt is reported thou didst eat strange flesh,\nWhich some did die to look on: and all this--\nIt wounds thine honour that I speak it now--\nWas borne so like a soldier, that thy cheek\nSo much as lank'd not.","sourceLine":"1.4.61","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis pity of him.","sourceLine":"1.4.78","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:1.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let his shames quickly\nDrive him to Rome: 'tis time we twain\nDid show ourselves i' the field; and to that end\nAssemble we immediate council: Pompey\nThrives in our idleness.","sourceLine":"1.4.79","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To-morrow, Caesar,\nI shall be furnish'd to inform you rightly\nBoth what by sea and land I can be able\nTo front this present time.","sourceLine":"1.4.84","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:1.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Till which encounter,\nIt is my business too. Farewell.","sourceLine":"1.4.88","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, my lord: what you shall know meantime\nOf stirs abroad, I shall beseech you, sir,\nTo let me be partaker.","sourceLine":"1.4.90","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:1.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Doubt not, sir;\nI knew it for my bond.","sourceLine":"1.4.93","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.4:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:1.4:17"}],"scene":4},{"id":"1.5","label":"Act 1 · SCENE V. Alexandria. CLEOPATRA's palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, CHARMIAN, IRAS, and MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Charmian!","sourceLine":"1.5.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam?","sourceLine":"1.5.2","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ha, ha!\nGive me to drink mandragora.","sourceLine":"1.5.3","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, madam?","sourceLine":"1.5.5","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That I might sleep out this great gap of time\nMy Antony is away.","sourceLine":"1.5.6","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You think of him too much.","sourceLine":"1.5.8","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, 'tis treason!","sourceLine":"1.5.9","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I trust, not so.","sourceLine":"1.5.10","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou, eunuch Mardian!","sourceLine":"1.5.11","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's your highness' pleasure?","sourceLine":"1.5.12","speaker":"MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not now to hear thee sing; I take no pleasure\nIn aught an eunuch has: 'tis well for thee,\nThat, being unseminar'd, thy freer thoughts\nMay not fly forth of Egypt. Hast thou affections?","sourceLine":"1.5.13","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, gracious madam.","sourceLine":"1.5.17","speaker":"MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed!","sourceLine":"1.5.18","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not in deed, madam; for I can do nothing\nBut what indeed is honest to be done:\nYet have I fierce affections, and think\nWhat Venus did with Mars.","sourceLine":"1.5.19","speaker":"MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Charmian,\nWhere think'st thou he is now? Stands he, or sits he?\nOr does he walk? or is he on his horse?\nO happy horse, to bear the weight of Antony!\nDo bravely, horse! for wot'st thou whom thou movest?\nThe demi-Atlas of this earth, the arm\nAnd burgonet of men. He's speaking now,\nOr murmuring 'Where's my serpent of old Nile?'\nFor so he calls me: now I feed myself\nWith most delicious poison. Think on me,\nThat am with Phoebus' amorous pinches black,\nAnd wrinkled deep in time? Broad-fronted Caesar,\nWhen thou wast here above the ground, I was\nA morsel for a monarch: and great Pompey\nWould stand and make his eyes grow in my brow;\nThere would he anchor his aspect and die\nWith looking on his life.","sourceLine":"1.5.23","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter ALEXAS, from OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:1.5:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sovereign of Egypt, hail!","sourceLine":"1.5.40","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How much unlike art thou Mark Antony!\nYet, coming from him, that great medicine hath\nWith his tinct gilded thee.\nHow goes it with my brave Mark Antony?","sourceLine":"1.5.41","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Last thing he did, dear queen,\nHe kiss'd,--the last of many doubled kisses,--\nThis orient pearl. His speech sticks in my heart.","sourceLine":"1.5.45","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mine ear must pluck it thence.","sourceLine":"1.5.48","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Good friend,' quoth he,\n'Say, the firm Roman to great Egypt sends\nThis treasure of an oyster; at whose foot,\nTo mend the petty present, I will piece\nHer opulent throne with kingdoms; all the east,\nSay thou, shall call her mistress.' So he nodded,\nAnd soberly did mount an arm-gaunt steed,\nWho neigh'd so high, that what I would have spoke\nWas beastly dumb'd by him.","sourceLine":"1.5.49","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, was he sad or merry?","sourceLine":"1.5.58","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Like to the time o' the year between the extremes\nOf hot and cold, he was nor sad nor merry.","sourceLine":"1.5.59","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.5:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O well-divided disposition! Note him,\nNote him good Charmian, 'tis the man; but note him:\nHe was not sad, for he would shine on those\nThat make their looks by his; he was not merry,\nWhich seem'd to tell them his remembrance lay\nIn Egypt with his joy; but between both:\nO heavenly mingle! Be'st thou sad or merry,\nThe violence of either thee becomes,\nSo does it no man else. Met'st thou my posts?","sourceLine":"1.5.61","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, madam, twenty several messengers:\nWhy do you send so thick?","sourceLine":"1.5.70","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:1.5:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who's born that day\nWhen I forget to send to Antony,\nShall die a beggar. Ink and paper, Charmian.\nWelcome, my good Alexas. Did I, Charmian,\nEver love Caesar so?","sourceLine":"1.5.72","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O that brave Caesar!","sourceLine":"1.5.77","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be choked with such another emphasis!\nSay, the brave Antony.","sourceLine":"1.5.78","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The valiant Caesar!","sourceLine":"1.5.80","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By Isis, I will give thee bloody teeth,\nIf thou with Caesar paragon again\nMy man of men.","sourceLine":"1.5.81","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By your most gracious pardon,\nI sing but after you.","sourceLine":"1.5.84","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:1.5:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My salad days,\nWhen I was green in judgment: cold in blood,\nTo say as I said then! But, come, away;\nGet me ink and paper:\nHe shall have every day a several greeting,\nOr I'll unpeople Egypt.","sourceLine":"1.5.86","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:1.5:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:1.5:34"}],"scene":5},{"id":"2.1","label":"Act 2 · SCENE I. Messina. POMPEY's house.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter POMPEY, MENECRATES, and MENAS, in warlike manner","id":"cleopatra:2.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If the great gods be just, they shall assist\nThe deeds of justest men.","sourceLine":"2.1.1","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Know, worthy Pompey,\nThat what they do delay, they not deny.","sourceLine":"2.1.3","speaker":"MENECRATES","id":"cleopatra:2.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whiles we are suitors to their throne, decays\nThe thing we sue for.","sourceLine":"2.1.5","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We, ignorant of ourselves,\nBeg often our own harms, which the wise powers\nDeny us for our good; so find we profit\nBy losing of our prayers.","sourceLine":"2.1.7","speaker":"MENECRATES","id":"cleopatra:2.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall do well:\nThe people love me, and the sea is mine;\nMy powers are crescent, and my auguring hope\nSays it will come to the full. Mark Antony\nIn Egypt sits at dinner, and will make\nNo wars without doors: Caesar gets money where\nHe loses hearts: Lepidus flatters both,\nOf both is flatter'd; but he neither loves,\nNor either cares for him.","sourceLine":"2.1.11","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar and Lepidus\nAre in the field: a mighty strength they carry.","sourceLine":"2.1.20","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where have you this? 'tis false.","sourceLine":"2.1.22","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From Silvius, sir.","sourceLine":"2.1.23","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He dreams: I know they are in Rome together,\nLooking for Antony. But all the charms of love,\nSalt Cleopatra, soften thy waned lip!\nLet witchcraft join with beauty, lust with both!\nTie up the libertine in a field of feasts,\nKeep his brain fuming; Epicurean cooks\nSharpen with cloyless sauce his appetite;\nThat sleep and feeding may prorogue his honour\nEven till a Lethe'd dulness!","sourceLine":"2.1.24","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter VARRIUS","id":"cleopatra:2.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, Varrius!","sourceLine":"2.1.33","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is most certain that I shall deliver:\nMark Antony is every hour in Rome\nExpected: since he went from Egypt 'tis\nA space for further travel.","sourceLine":"2.1.34","speaker":"VARRIUS","id":"cleopatra:2.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I could have given less matter\nA better ear. Menas, I did not think\nThis amorous surfeiter would have donn'd his helm\nFor such a petty war: his soldiership\nIs twice the other twain: but let us rear\nThe higher our opinion, that our stirring\nCan from the lap of Egypt's widow pluck\nThe ne'er-lust-wearied Antony.","sourceLine":"2.1.38","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot hope\nCaesar and Antony shall well greet together:\nHis wife that's dead did trespasses to Caesar;\nHis brother warr'd upon him; although, I think,\nNot moved by Antony.","sourceLine":"2.1.46","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know not, Menas,\nHow lesser enmities may give way to greater.\nWere't not that we stand up against them all,\n'Twere pregnant they should square between\nthemselves;\nFor they have entertained cause enough\nTo draw their swords: but how the fear of us\nMay cement their divisions and bind up\nThe petty difference, we yet not know.\nBe't as our gods will have't! It only stands\nOur lives upon to use our strongest hands.\nCome, Menas.","sourceLine":"2.1.51","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.1:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:2.1:17"}],"scene":1},{"id":"2.2","label":"Act 2 · SCENE II. Rome. The house of LEPIDUS.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS and LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good Enobarbus, 'tis a worthy deed,\nAnd shall become you well, to entreat your captain\nTo soft and gentle speech.","sourceLine":"2.2.1","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall entreat him\nTo answer like himself: if Caesar move him,\nLet Antony look over Caesar's head\nAnd speak as loud as Mars. By Jupiter,\nWere I the wearer of Antonius' beard,\nI would not shave't to-day.","sourceLine":"2.2.4","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis not a time\nFor private stomaching.","sourceLine":"2.2.10","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Every time\nServes for the matter that is then born in't.","sourceLine":"2.2.12","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But small to greater matters must give way.","sourceLine":"2.2.14","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not if the small come first.","sourceLine":"2.2.15","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your speech is passion:\nBut, pray you, stir no embers up. Here comes\nThe noble Antony.","sourceLine":"2.2.16","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and VENTIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And yonder, Caesar.","sourceLine":"2.2.19","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, MECAENAS, and AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If we compose well here, to Parthia:\nHark, Ventidius.","sourceLine":"2.2.20","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do not know,\nMecaenas; ask Agrippa.","sourceLine":"2.2.22","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Noble friends,\nThat which combined us was most great, and let not\nA leaner action rend us. What's amiss,\nMay it be gently heard: when we debate\nOur trivial difference loud, we do commit\nMurder in healing wounds: then, noble partners,\nThe rather, for I earnestly beseech,\nTouch you the sourest points with sweetest terms,\nNor curstness grow to the matter.","sourceLine":"2.2.24","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis spoken well.\nWere we before our armies, and to fight.\nI should do thus.","sourceLine":"2.2.33","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish","id":"cleopatra:2.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome to Rome.","sourceLine":"2.2.36","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thank you.","sourceLine":"2.2.37","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sit.","sourceLine":"2.2.38","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sit, sir.","sourceLine":"2.2.39","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, then.","sourceLine":"2.2.40","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I learn, you take things ill which are not so,\nOr being, concern you not.","sourceLine":"2.2.41","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must be laugh'd at,\nIf, or for nothing or a little, I\nShould say myself offended, and with you\nChiefly i' the world; more laugh'd at, that I should\nOnce name you derogately, when to sound your name\nIt not concern'd me.","sourceLine":"2.2.43","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My being in Egypt, Caesar,\nWhat was't to you?","sourceLine":"2.2.49","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more than my residing here at Rome\nMight be to you in Egypt: yet, if you there\nDid practise on my state, your being in Egypt\nMight be my question.","sourceLine":"2.2.51","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How intend you, practised?","sourceLine":"2.2.55","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You may be pleased to catch at mine intent\nBy what did here befal me. Your wife and brother\nMade wars upon me; and their contestation\nWas theme for you, you were the word of war.","sourceLine":"2.2.56","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You do mistake your business; my brother never\nDid urge me in his act: I did inquire it;\nAnd have my learning from some true reports,\nThat drew their swords with you. Did he not rather\nDiscredit my authority with yours;\nAnd make the wars alike against my stomach,\nHaving alike your cause? Of this my letters\nBefore did satisfy you. If you'll patch a quarrel,\nAs matter whole you have not to make it with,\nIt must not be with this.","sourceLine":"2.2.60","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You praise yourself\nBy laying defects of judgment to me; but\nYou patch'd up your excuses.","sourceLine":"2.2.70","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not so, not so;\nI know you could not lack, I am certain on't,\nVery necessity of this thought, that I,\nYour partner in the cause 'gainst which he fought,\nCould not with graceful eyes attend those wars\nWhich fronted mine own peace. As for my wife,\nI would you had her spirit in such another:\nThe third o' the world is yours; which with a snaffle\nYou may pace easy, but not such a wife.","sourceLine":"2.2.73","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would we had all such wives, that the men might go\nto wars with the women!","sourceLine":"2.2.82","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So much uncurbable, her garboils, Caesar\nMade out of her impatience, which not wanted\nShrewdness of policy too, I grieving grant\nDid you too much disquiet: for that you must\nBut say, I could not help it.","sourceLine":"2.2.84","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I wrote to you\nWhen rioting in Alexandria; you\nDid pocket up my letters, and with taunts\nDid gibe my missive out of audience.","sourceLine":"2.2.89","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir,\nHe fell upon me ere admitted: then\nThree kings I had newly feasted, and did want\nOf what I was i' the morning: but next day\nI told him of myself; which was as much\nAs to have ask'd him pardon. Let this fellow\nBe nothing of our strife; if we contend,\nOut of our question wipe him.","sourceLine":"2.2.93","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have broken\nThe article of your oath; which you shall never\nHave tongue to charge me with.","sourceLine":"2.2.101","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Soft, Caesar!","sourceLine":"2.2.104","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No,\nLepidus, let him speak:\nThe honour is sacred which he talks on now,\nSupposing that I lack'd it. But, on, Caesar;\nThe article of my oath.","sourceLine":"2.2.105","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To lend me arms and aid when I required them;\nThe which you both denied.","sourceLine":"2.2.110","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Neglected, rather;\nAnd then when poison'd hours had bound me up\nFrom mine own knowledge. As nearly as I may,\nI'll play the penitent to you: but mine honesty\nShall not make poor my greatness, nor my power\nWork without it. Truth is, that Fulvia,\nTo have me out of Egypt, made wars here;\nFor which myself, the ignorant motive, do\nSo far ask pardon as befits mine honour\nTo stoop in such a case.","sourceLine":"2.2.112","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis noble spoken.","sourceLine":"2.2.122","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If it might please you, to enforce no further\nThe griefs between ye: to forget them quite\nWere to remember that the present need\nSpeaks to atone you.","sourceLine":"2.2.123","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Worthily spoken, Mecaenas.","sourceLine":"2.2.127","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or, if you borrow one another's love for the\ninstant, you may, when you hear no more words of\nPompey, return it again: you shall have time to\nwrangle in when you have nothing else to do.","sourceLine":"2.2.128","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou art a soldier only: speak no more.","sourceLine":"2.2.132","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That truth should be silent I had almost forgot.","sourceLine":"2.2.133","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You wrong this presence; therefore speak no more.","sourceLine":"2.2.134","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go to, then; your considerate stone.","sourceLine":"2.2.135","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do not much dislike the matter, but\nThe manner of his speech; for't cannot be\nWe shall remain in friendship, our conditions\nSo differing in their acts. Yet if I knew\nWhat hoop should hold us stanch, from edge to edge\nO' the world I would pursue it.","sourceLine":"2.2.136","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me leave, Caesar,--","sourceLine":"2.2.142","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Speak, Agrippa.","sourceLine":"2.2.143","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hast a sister by the mother's side,\nAdmired Octavia: great Mark Antony\nIs now a widower.","sourceLine":"2.2.144","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say not so, Agrippa:\nIf Cleopatra heard you, your reproof\nWere well deserved of rashness.","sourceLine":"2.2.147","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am not married, Caesar: let me hear\nAgrippa further speak.","sourceLine":"2.2.150","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To hold you in perpetual amity,\nTo make you brothers, and to knit your hearts\nWith an unslipping knot, take Antony\nOctavia to his wife; whose beauty claims\nNo worse a husband than the best of men;\nWhose virtue and whose general graces speak\nThat which none else can utter. By this marriage,\nAll little jealousies, which now seem great,\nAnd all great fears, which now import their dangers,\nWould then be nothing: truths would be tales,\nWhere now half tales be truths: her love to both\nWould, each to other and all loves to both,\nDraw after her. Pardon what I have spoke;\nFor 'tis a studied, not a present thought,\nBy duty ruminated.","sourceLine":"2.2.152","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will Caesar speak?","sourceLine":"2.2.167","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not till he hears how Antony is touch'd\nWith what is spoke already.","sourceLine":"2.2.168","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What power is in Agrippa,\nIf I would say, 'Agrippa, be it so,'\nTo make this good?","sourceLine":"2.2.170","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The power of Caesar, and\nHis power unto Octavia.","sourceLine":"2.2.173","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May I never\nTo this good purpose, that so fairly shows,\nDream of impediment! Let me have thy hand:\nFurther this act of grace: and from this hour\nThe heart of brothers govern in our loves\nAnd sway our great designs!","sourceLine":"2.2.175","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is my hand.\nA sister I bequeath you, whom no brother\nDid ever love so dearly: let her live\nTo join our kingdoms and our hearts; and never\nFly off our loves again!","sourceLine":"2.2.181","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Happily, amen!","sourceLine":"2.2.186","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did not think to draw my sword 'gainst Pompey;\nFor he hath laid strange courtesies and great\nOf late upon me: I must thank him only,\nLest my remembrance suffer ill report;\nAt heel of that, defy him.","sourceLine":"2.2.187","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Time calls upon's:\nOf us must Pompey presently be sought,\nOr else he seeks out us.","sourceLine":"2.2.192","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where lies he?","sourceLine":"2.2.195","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"About the mount Misenum.","sourceLine":"2.2.196","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is his strength by land?","sourceLine":"2.2.197","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Great and increasing: but by sea\nHe is an absolute master.","sourceLine":"2.2.198","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So is the fame.\nWould we had spoke together! Haste we for it:\nYet, ere we put ourselves in arms, dispatch we\nThe business we have talk'd of.","sourceLine":"2.2.200","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With most gladness:\nAnd do invite you to my sister's view,\nWhither straight I'll lead you.","sourceLine":"2.2.204","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us, Lepidus,\nNot lack your company.","sourceLine":"2.2.207","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.2:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Noble Antony,\nNot sickness should detain me.","sourceLine":"2.2.209","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt OCTAVIUS CAESAR, MARK ANTONY, and LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome from Egypt, sir.","sourceLine":"2.2.211","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Half the heart of Caesar, worthy Mecaenas! My\nhonourable friend, Agrippa!","sourceLine":"2.2.212","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good Enobarbus!","sourceLine":"2.2.214","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We have cause to be glad that matters are so well\ndigested. You stayed well by 't in Egypt.","sourceLine":"2.2.215","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, sir; we did sleep day out of countenance, and\nmade the night light with drinking.","sourceLine":"2.2.217","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Eight wild-boars roasted whole at a breakfast, and\nbut twelve persons there; is this true?","sourceLine":"2.2.219","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This was but as a fly by an eagle: we had much more\nmonstrous matter of feast, which worthily deserved noting.","sourceLine":"2.2.221","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She's a most triumphant lady, if report be square to\nher.","sourceLine":"2.2.223","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When she first met Mark Antony, she pursed up\nhis heart, upon the river of Cydnus.","sourceLine":"2.2.225","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There she appeared indeed; or my reporter devised\nwell for her.","sourceLine":"2.2.227","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will tell you.\nThe barge she sat in, like a burnish'd throne,\nBurn'd on the water: the poop was beaten gold;\nPurple the sails, and so perfumed that\nThe winds were love-sick with them; the oars were silver,\nWhich to the tune of flutes kept stroke, and made\nThe water which they beat to follow faster,\nAs amorous of their strokes. For her own person,\nIt beggar'd all description: she did lie\nIn her pavilion--cloth-of-gold of tissue--\nO'er-picturing that Venus where we see\nThe fancy outwork nature: on each side her\nStood pretty dimpled boys, like smiling Cupids,\nWith divers-colour'd fans, whose wind did seem\nTo glow the delicate cheeks which they did cool,\nAnd what they undid did.","sourceLine":"2.2.229","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, rare for Antony!","sourceLine":"2.2.245","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Her gentlewomen, like the Nereides,\nSo many mermaids, tended her i' the eyes,\nAnd made their bends adornings: at the helm\nA seeming mermaid steers: the silken tackle\nSwell with the touches of those flower-soft hands,\nThat yarely frame the office. From the barge\nA strange invisible perfume hits the sense\nOf the adjacent wharfs. The city cast\nHer people out upon her; and Antony,\nEnthroned i' the market-place, did sit alone,\nWhistling to the air; which, but for vacancy,\nHad gone to gaze on Cleopatra too,\nAnd made a gap in nature.","sourceLine":"2.2.246","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rare Egyptian!","sourceLine":"2.2.259","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon her landing, Antony sent to her,\nInvited her to supper: she replied,\nIt should be better he became her guest;\nWhich she entreated: our courteous Antony,\nWhom ne'er the word of 'No' woman heard speak,\nBeing barber'd ten times o'er, goes to the feast,\nAnd for his ordinary pays his heart\nFor what his eyes eat only.","sourceLine":"2.2.260","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Royal wench!\nShe made great Caesar lay his sword to bed:\nHe plough'd her, and she cropp'd.","sourceLine":"2.2.268","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I saw her once\nHop forty paces through the public street;\nAnd having lost her breath, she spoke, and panted,\nThat she did make defect perfection,\nAnd, breathless, power breathe forth.","sourceLine":"2.2.271","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now Antony must leave her utterly.","sourceLine":"2.2.276","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Never; he will not:\nAge cannot wither her, nor custom stale\nHer infinite variety: other women cloy\nThe appetites they feed: but she makes hungry\nWhere most she satisfies; for vilest things\nBecome themselves in her: that the holy priests\nBless her when she is riggish.","sourceLine":"2.2.277","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If beauty, wisdom, modesty, can settle\nThe heart of Antony, Octavia is\nA blessed lottery to him.","sourceLine":"2.2.284","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us go.\nGood Enobarbus, make yourself my guest\nWhilst you abide here.","sourceLine":"2.2.287","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.2:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Humbly, sir, I thank you.","sourceLine":"2.2.290","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.2:94"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:2.2:95"}],"scene":2},{"id":"2.3","label":"Act 2 · SCENE III. The same. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's house.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY, OCTAVIUS CAESAR, OCTAVIA between them, and Attendants","id":"cleopatra:2.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The world and my great office will sometimes\nDivide me from your bosom.","sourceLine":"2.3.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All which time\nBefore the gods my knee shall bow my prayers\nTo them for you.","sourceLine":"2.3.3","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:2.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good night, sir. My Octavia,\nRead not my blemishes in the world's report:\nI have not kept my square; but that to come\nShall all be done by the rule. Good night, dear lady.\nGood night, sir.","sourceLine":"2.3.6","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good night.","sourceLine":"2.3.11","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.3:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt OCTAVIUS CAESAR and OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:2.3:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:2.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, sirrah; you do wish yourself in Egypt?","sourceLine":"2.3.12","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would I had never come from thence, nor you Thither!","sourceLine":"2.3.13","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:2.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you can, your reason?","sourceLine":"2.3.14","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I see it in\nMy motion, have it not in my tongue: but yet\nHie you to Egypt again.","sourceLine":"2.3.15","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:2.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say to me,\nWhose fortunes shall rise higher, Caesar's or mine?","sourceLine":"2.3.18","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar's.\nTherefore, O Antony, stay not by his side:\nThy demon, that's thy spirit which keeps thee, is\nNoble, courageous high, unmatchable,\nWhere Caesar's is not; but, near him, thy angel\nBecomes a fear, as being o'erpower'd: therefore\nMake space enough between you.","sourceLine":"2.3.20","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:2.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Speak this no more.","sourceLine":"2.3.27","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To none but thee; no more, but when to thee.\nIf thou dost play with him at any game,\nThou art sure to lose; and, of that natural luck,\nHe beats thee 'gainst the odds: thy lustre thickens,\nWhen he shines by: I say again, thy spirit\nIs all afraid to govern thee near him;\nBut, he away, 'tis noble.","sourceLine":"2.3.28","speaker":"Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:2.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Get thee gone:\nSay to Ventidius I would speak with him:","sourceLine":"2.3.35","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Soothsayer","id":"cleopatra:2.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He shall to Parthia. Be it art or hap,\nHe hath spoken true: the very dice obey him;\nAnd in our sports my better cunning faints\nUnder his chance: if we draw lots, he speeds;\nHis cocks do win the battle still of mine,\nWhen it is all to nought; and his quails ever\nBeat mine, inhoop'd, at odds. I will to Egypt:\nAnd though I make this marriage for my peace,\nI' the east my pleasure lies.","sourceLine":"2.3.37","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter VENTIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:2.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, come, Ventidius,\nYou must to Parthia: your commission's ready;\nFollow me, and receive't.","sourceLine":"2.3.46","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.3:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:2.3:21"}],"scene":3},{"id":"2.4","label":"Act 2 · SCENE IV. The same. A street.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter LEPIDUS, MECAENAS, and AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Trouble yourselves no further: pray you, hasten\nYour generals after.","sourceLine":"2.4.1","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, Mark Antony\nWill e'en but kiss Octavia, and we'll follow.","sourceLine":"2.4.3","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Till I shall see you in your soldier's dress,\nWhich will become you both, farewell.","sourceLine":"2.4.5","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We shall,\nAs I conceive the journey, be at the Mount\nBefore you, Lepidus.","sourceLine":"2.4.7","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your way is shorter;\nMy purposes do draw me much about:\nYou'll win two days upon me.","sourceLine":"2.4.10","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, good success!","sourceLine":"2.4.13","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:2.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell.","sourceLine":"2.4.14","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.4:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:2.4:9"}],"scene":4},{"id":"2.5","label":"Act 2 · SCENE V. Alexandria. CLEOPATRA's palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, CHARMIAN, IRAS, and ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:2.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me some music; music, moody food\nOf us that trade in love.","sourceLine":"2.5.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The music, ho!","sourceLine":"2.5.3","speaker":"Attendants","id":"cleopatra:2.5:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let it alone; let's to billiards: come, Charmian.","sourceLine":"2.5.4","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My arm is sore; best play with Mardian.","sourceLine":"2.5.5","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As well a woman with an eunuch play'd\nAs with a woman. Come, you'll play with me, sir?","sourceLine":"2.5.6","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As well as I can, madam.","sourceLine":"2.5.8","speaker":"MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And when good will is show'd, though't come\ntoo short,\nThe actor may plead pardon. I'll none now:\nGive me mine angle; we'll to the river: there,\nMy music playing far off, I will betray\nTawny-finn'd fishes; my bended hook shall pierce\nTheir slimy jaws; and, as I draw them up,\nI'll think them every one an Antony,\nAnd say 'Ah, ha! you're caught.'","sourceLine":"2.5.9","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Twas merry when\nYou wager'd on your angling; when your diver\nDid hang a salt-fish on his hook, which he\nWith fervency drew up.","sourceLine":"2.5.18","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That time,--O times!--\nI laugh'd him out of patience; and that night\nI laugh'd him into patience; and next morn,\nEre the ninth hour, I drunk him to his bed;\nThen put my tires and mantles on him, whilst\nI wore his sword Philippan.","sourceLine":"2.5.22","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, from Italy\nRam thou thy fruitful tidings in mine ears,\nThat long time have been barren.","sourceLine":"2.5.28","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, madam,--","sourceLine":"2.5.31","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Antonius dead!--If thou say so, villain,\nThou kill'st thy mistress: but well and free,\nIf thou so yield him, there is gold, and here\nMy bluest veins to kiss; a hand that kings\nHave lipp'd, and trembled kissing.","sourceLine":"2.5.32","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"First, madam, he is well.","sourceLine":"2.5.37","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, there's more gold.\nBut, sirrah, mark, we use\nTo say the dead are well: bring it to that,\nThe gold I give thee will I melt and pour\nDown thy ill-uttering throat.","sourceLine":"2.5.38","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good madam, hear me.","sourceLine":"2.5.43","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, go to, I will;\nBut there's no goodness in thy face: if Antony\nBe free and healthful,--so tart a favour\nTo trumpet such good tidings! If not well,\nThou shouldst come like a Fury crown'd with snakes,\nNot like a formal man.","sourceLine":"2.5.44","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will't please you hear me?","sourceLine":"2.5.50","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have a mind to strike thee ere thou speak'st:\nYet if thou say Antony lives, is well,\nOr friends with Caesar, or not captive to him,\nI'll set thee in a shower of gold, and hail\nRich pearls upon thee.","sourceLine":"2.5.51","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, he's well.","sourceLine":"2.5.56","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well said.","sourceLine":"2.5.57","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And friends with Caesar.","sourceLine":"2.5.58","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou'rt an honest man.","sourceLine":"2.5.59","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar and he are greater friends than ever.","sourceLine":"2.5.60","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make thee a fortune from me.","sourceLine":"2.5.61","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But yet, madam,--","sourceLine":"2.5.62","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do not like 'But yet,' it does allay\nThe good precedence; fie upon 'But yet'!\n'But yet' is as a gaoler to bring forth\nSome monstrous malefactor. Prithee, friend,\nPour out the pack of matter to mine ear,\nThe good and bad together: he's friends with Caesar:\nIn state of health thou say'st; and thou say'st free.","sourceLine":"2.5.63","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Free, madam! no; I made no such report:\nHe's bound unto Octavia.","sourceLine":"2.5.70","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For what good turn?","sourceLine":"2.5.72","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For the best turn i' the bed.","sourceLine":"2.5.73","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am pale, Charmian.","sourceLine":"2.5.74","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, he's married to Octavia.","sourceLine":"2.5.75","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The most infectious pestilence upon thee!","sourceLine":"2.5.76","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Strikes him down","id":"cleopatra:2.5:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good madam, patience.","sourceLine":"2.5.77","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say you? Hence,","sourceLine":"2.5.78","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:38"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Strikes him again","id":"cleopatra:2.5:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Horrible villain! or I'll spurn thine eyes\nLike balls before me; I'll unhair thy head:","sourceLine":"2.5.79","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"She hales him up and down","id":"cleopatra:2.5:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou shalt be whipp'd with wire, and stew'd in brine,\nSmarting in lingering pickle.","sourceLine":"2.5.81","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gracious madam,\nI that do bring the news made not the match.","sourceLine":"2.5.83","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say 'tis not so, a province I will give thee,\nAnd make thy fortunes proud: the blow thou hadst\nShall make thy peace for moving me to rage;\nAnd I will boot thee with what gift beside\nThy modesty can beg.","sourceLine":"2.5.85","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He's married, madam.","sourceLine":"2.5.90","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rogue, thou hast lived too long.","sourceLine":"2.5.91","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Draws a knife","id":"cleopatra:2.5:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, then I'll run.\nWhat mean you, madam? I have made no fault.","sourceLine":"2.5.92","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:2.5:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good madam, keep yourself within yourself:\nThe man is innocent.","sourceLine":"2.5.94","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Some innocents 'scape not the thunderbolt.\nMelt Egypt into Nile! and kindly creatures\nTurn all to serpents! Call the slave again:\nThough I am mad, I will not bite him: call.","sourceLine":"2.5.96","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is afeard to come.","sourceLine":"2.5.100","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will not hurt him.","sourceLine":"2.5.101","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:53"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These hands do lack nobility, that they strike\nA meaner than myself; since I myself\nHave given myself the cause.","sourceLine":"2.5.102","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:55"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter CHARMIAN and Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come hither, sir.\nThough it be honest, it is never good\nTo bring bad news: give to a gracious message.\nAn host of tongues; but let ill tidings tell\nThemselves when they be felt.","sourceLine":"2.5.105","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have done my duty.","sourceLine":"2.5.110","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is he married?\nI cannot hate thee worser than I do,\nIf thou again say 'Yes.'","sourceLine":"2.5.111","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He's married, madam.","sourceLine":"2.5.114","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods confound thee! dost thou hold there still?","sourceLine":"2.5.115","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Should I lie, madam?","sourceLine":"2.5.116","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, I would thou didst,\nSo half my Egypt were submerged and made\nA cistern for scaled snakes! Go, get thee hence:\nHadst thou Narcissus in thy face, to me\nThou wouldst appear most ugly. He is married?","sourceLine":"2.5.117","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I crave your highness' pardon.","sourceLine":"2.5.122","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is married?","sourceLine":"2.5.123","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take no offence that I would not offend you:\nTo punish me for what you make me do.\nSeems much unequal: he's married to Octavia.","sourceLine":"2.5.124","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, that his fault should make a knave of thee,\nThat art not what thou'rt sure of! Get thee hence:\nThe merchandise which thou hast brought from Rome\nAre all too dear for me: lie they upon thy hand,\nAnd be undone by 'em!","sourceLine":"2.5.127","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:67"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Messenger","id":"cleopatra:2.5:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good your highness, patience.","sourceLine":"2.5.132","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In praising Antony, I have dispraised Caesar.","sourceLine":"2.5.133","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Many times, madam.","sourceLine":"2.5.134","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am paid for't now.\nLead me from hence:\nI faint: O Iras, Charmian! 'tis no matter.\nGo to the fellow, good Alexas; bid him\nReport the feature of Octavia, her years,\nHer inclination, let him not leave out\nThe colour of her hair: bring me word quickly.","sourceLine":"2.5.135","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:72"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:2.5:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him for ever go:--let him not--Charmian,\nThough he be painted one way like a Gorgon,\nThe other way's a Mars. Bid you Alexas","sourceLine":"2.5.142","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:74"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:2.5:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bring me word how tall she is. Pity me, Charmian,\nBut do not speak to me. Lead me to my chamber.","sourceLine":"2.5.145","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:2.5:76"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:2.5:77"}],"scene":5},{"id":"2.6","label":"Act 2 · SCENE VI. Near Misenum.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter POMPEY and MENAS at one door, with drum and trumpet: at another, OCTAVIUS CAESAR, MARK ANTONY, LEPIDUS, DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS, MECAENAS, with Soldiers marching","id":"cleopatra:2.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your hostages I have, so have you mine;\nAnd we shall talk before we fight.","sourceLine":"2.6.1","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most meet\nThat first we come to words; and therefore have we\nOur written purposes before us sent;\nWhich, if thou hast consider'd, let us know\nIf 'twill tie up thy discontented sword,\nAnd carry back to Sicily much tall youth\nThat else must perish here.","sourceLine":"2.6.3","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.6:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To you all three,\nThe senators alone of this great world,\nChief factors for the gods, I do not know\nWherefore my father should revengers want,\nHaving a son and friends; since Julius Caesar,\nWho at Philippi the good Brutus ghosted,\nThere saw you labouring for him. What was't\nThat moved pale Cassius to conspire; and what\nMade the all-honour'd, honest Roman, Brutus,\nWith the arm'd rest, courtiers and beauteous freedom,\nTo drench the Capitol; but that they would\nHave one man but a man? And that is it\nHath made me rig my navy; at whose burthen\nThe anger'd ocean foams; with which I meant\nTo scourge the ingratitude that despiteful Rome\nCast on my noble father.","sourceLine":"2.6.10","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take your time.","sourceLine":"2.6.26","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.6:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou canst not fear us, Pompey, with thy sails;\nWe'll speak with thee at sea: at land, thou know'st\nHow much we do o'er-count thee.","sourceLine":"2.6.27","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At land, indeed,\nThou dost o'er-count me of my father's house:\nBut, since the cuckoo builds not for himself,\nRemain in't as thou mayst.","sourceLine":"2.6.30","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be pleased to tell us--\nFor this is from the present--how you take\nThe offers we have sent you.","sourceLine":"2.6.34","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's the point.","sourceLine":"2.6.37","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.6:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which do not be entreated to, but weigh\nWhat it is worth embraced.","sourceLine":"2.6.38","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And what may follow,\nTo try a larger fortune.","sourceLine":"2.6.40","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.6:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have made me offer\nOf Sicily, Sardinia; and I must\nRid all the sea of pirates; then, to send\nMeasures of wheat to Rome; this 'greed upon\nTo part with unhack'd edges, and bear back\nOur targes undinted.","sourceLine":"2.6.42","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's our offer.","sourceLine":"2.6.48","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Know, then,\nI came before you here a man prepared\nTo take this offer: but Mark Antony\nPut me to some impatience: though I lose\nThe praise of it by telling, you must know,\nWhen Caesar and your brother were at blows,\nYour mother came to Sicily and did find\nHer welcome friendly.","sourceLine":"2.6.49","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have heard it, Pompey;\nAnd am well studied for a liberal thanks\nWhich I do owe you.","sourceLine":"2.6.57","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me have your hand:\nI did not think, sir, to have met you here.","sourceLine":"2.6.60","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The beds i' the east are soft; and thanks to you,\nThat call'd me timelier than my purpose hither;\nFor I have gain'd by 't.","sourceLine":"2.6.62","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Since I saw you last,\nThere is a change upon you.","sourceLine":"2.6.65","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.6:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, I know not\nWhat counts harsh fortune casts upon my face;\nBut in my bosom shall she never come,\nTo make my heart her vassal.","sourceLine":"2.6.67","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well met here.","sourceLine":"2.6.71","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope so, Lepidus. Thus we are agreed:\nI crave our composition may be written,\nAnd seal'd between us.","sourceLine":"2.6.72","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's the next to do.","sourceLine":"2.6.75","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.6:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll feast each other ere we part; and let's\nDraw lots who shall begin.","sourceLine":"2.6.76","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That will I, Pompey.","sourceLine":"2.6.78","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, Antony, take the lot: but, first\nOr last, your fine Egyptian cookery\nShall have the fame. I have heard that Julius Caesar\nGrew fat with feasting there.","sourceLine":"2.6.79","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have heard much.","sourceLine":"2.6.83","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have fair meanings, sir.","sourceLine":"2.6.84","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And fair words to them.","sourceLine":"2.6.85","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then so much have I heard:\nAnd I have heard, Apollodorus carried--","sourceLine":"2.6.86","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more of that: he did so.","sourceLine":"2.6.88","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, I pray you?","sourceLine":"2.6.89","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A certain queen to Caesar in a mattress.","sourceLine":"2.6.90","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know thee now: how farest thou, soldier?","sourceLine":"2.6.91","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well;\nAnd well am like to do; for, I perceive,\nFour feasts are toward.","sourceLine":"2.6.92","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me shake thy hand;\nI never hated thee: I have seen thee fight,\nWhen I have envied thy behavior.","sourceLine":"2.6.95","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir,\nI never loved you much; but I ha' praised ye,\nWhen you have well deserved ten times as much\nAs I have said you did.","sourceLine":"2.6.98","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Enjoy thy plainness,\nIt nothing ill becomes thee.\nAboard my galley I invite you all:\nWill you lead, lords?","sourceLine":"2.6.102","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Show us the way, sir.","sourceLine":"2.6.106","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come.","sourceLine":"2.6.107","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.6:39"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but MENAS and ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Thy father, Pompey, would ne'er have\nmade this treaty.--You and I have known, sir.","sourceLine":"2.6.108","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At sea, I think.","sourceLine":"2.6.110","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We have, sir.","sourceLine":"2.6.111","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have done well by water.","sourceLine":"2.6.112","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And you by land.","sourceLine":"2.6.113","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will praise any man that will praise me; though it\ncannot be denied what I have done by land.","sourceLine":"2.6.114","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor what I have done by water.","sourceLine":"2.6.116","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, something you can deny for your own\nsafety: you have been a great thief by sea.","sourceLine":"2.6.117","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And you by land.","sourceLine":"2.6.119","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There I deny my land service. But give me your\nhand, Menas: if our eyes had authority, here they\nmight take two thieves kissing.","sourceLine":"2.6.120","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All men's faces are true, whatsome'er their hands are.","sourceLine":"2.6.123","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But there is never a fair woman has a true face.","sourceLine":"2.6.124","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No slander; they steal hearts.","sourceLine":"2.6.125","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We came hither to fight with you.","sourceLine":"2.6.126","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For my part, I am sorry it is turned to a drinking.\nPompey doth this day laugh away his fortune.","sourceLine":"2.6.127","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If he do, sure, he cannot weep't back again.","sourceLine":"2.6.129","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You've said, sir. We looked not for Mark Antony\nhere: pray you, is he married to Cleopatra?","sourceLine":"2.6.130","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar's sister is called Octavia.","sourceLine":"2.6.132","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"True, sir; she was the wife of Caius Marcellus.","sourceLine":"2.6.133","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But she is now the wife of Marcus Antonius.","sourceLine":"2.6.134","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray ye, sir?","sourceLine":"2.6.135","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis true.","sourceLine":"2.6.136","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then is Caesar and he for ever knit together.","sourceLine":"2.6.137","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I were bound to divine of this unity, I would\nnot prophesy so.","sourceLine":"2.6.138","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think the policy of that purpose made more in the\nmarriage than the love of the parties.","sourceLine":"2.6.140","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think so too. But you shall find, the band that\nseems to tie their friendship together will be the\nvery strangler of their amity: Octavia is of a\nholy, cold, and still conversation.","sourceLine":"2.6.142","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who would not have his wife so?","sourceLine":"2.6.146","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not he that himself is not so; which is Mark Antony.\nHe will to his Egyptian dish again: then shall the\nsighs of Octavia blow the fire up in Caesar; and, as\nI said before, that which is the strength of their\namity shall prove the immediate author of their\nvariance. Antony will use his affection where it is:\nhe married but his occasion here.","sourceLine":"2.6.147","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And thus it may be. Come, sir, will you aboard?\nI have a health for you.","sourceLine":"2.6.154","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall take it, sir: we have used our throats in Egypt.","sourceLine":"2.6.156","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, let's away.","sourceLine":"2.6.157","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.6:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:2.6:72"}],"scene":6},{"id":"2.7","label":"Act 2 · SCENE VII. On board POMPEY's galley, off Misenum.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Music plays. Enter two or three Servants with a banquet","id":"cleopatra:2.7:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here they'll be, man. Some o' their plants are\nill-rooted already: the least wind i' the world\nwill blow them down.","sourceLine":"2.7.1","speaker":"First Servant","id":"cleopatra:2.7:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lepidus is high-coloured.","sourceLine":"2.7.4","speaker":"Second Servant","id":"cleopatra:2.7:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They have made him drink alms-drink.","sourceLine":"2.7.5","speaker":"First Servant","id":"cleopatra:2.7:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As they pinch one another by the disposition, he\ncries out 'No more;' reconciles them to his\nentreaty, and himself to the drink.","sourceLine":"2.7.6","speaker":"Second Servant","id":"cleopatra:2.7:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But it raises the greater war between him and\nhis discretion.","sourceLine":"2.7.9","speaker":"First Servant","id":"cleopatra:2.7:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, this is to have a name in great men's\nfellowship: I had as lief have a reed that will do\nme no service as a partisan I could not heave.","sourceLine":"2.7.11","speaker":"Second Servant","id":"cleopatra:2.7:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To be called into a huge sphere, and not to be seen\nto move in't, are the holes where eyes should be,\nwhich pitifully disaster the cheeks.","sourceLine":"2.7.14","speaker":"First Servant","id":"cleopatra:2.7:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"A sennet sounded. Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, MARK ANTONY, LEPIDUS, POMPEY, AGRIPPA, MECAENAS, DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS, MENAS, with other captains","id":"cleopatra:2.7:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To OCTAVIUS CAESAR] Thus do they, sir: they take\nthe flow o' the Nile\nBy certain scales i' the pyramid; they know,\nBy the height, the lowness, or the mean, if dearth\nOr foison follow: the higher Nilus swells,\nThe more it promises: as it ebbs, the seedsman\nUpon the slime and ooze scatters his grain,\nAnd shortly comes to harvest.","sourceLine":"2.7.17","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You've strange serpents there.","sourceLine":"2.7.25","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, Lepidus.","sourceLine":"2.7.26","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your serpent of Egypt is bred now of your mud by the\noperation of your sun: so is your crocodile.","sourceLine":"2.7.27","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are so.","sourceLine":"2.7.29","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sit,--and some wine! A health to Lepidus!","sourceLine":"2.7.30","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am not so well as I should be, but I'll ne'er out.","sourceLine":"2.7.31","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not till you have slept; I fear me you'll be in till then.","sourceLine":"2.7.32","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, certainly, I have heard the Ptolemies'\npyramises are very goodly things; without\ncontradiction, I have heard that.","sourceLine":"2.7.33","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to POMPEY] Pompey, a word.","sourceLine":"2.7.36","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to MENAS] Say in mine ear:\nwhat is't?","sourceLine":"2.7.37","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to POMPEY] Forsake thy seat, I do beseech\nthee, captain,\nAnd hear me speak a word.","sourceLine":"2.7.39","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to MENAS] Forbear me till anon.\nThis wine for Lepidus!","sourceLine":"2.7.42","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What manner o' thing is your crocodile?","sourceLine":"2.7.44","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is shaped, sir, like itself; and it is as broad\nas it hath breadth: it is just so high as it is,\nand moves with its own organs: it lives by that\nwhich nourisheth it; and the elements once out of\nit, it transmigrates.","sourceLine":"2.7.45","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What colour is it of?","sourceLine":"2.7.50","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Of it own colour too.","sourceLine":"2.7.51","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis a strange serpent.","sourceLine":"2.7.52","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis so. And the tears of it are wet.","sourceLine":"2.7.53","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will this description satisfy him?","sourceLine":"2.7.54","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.7:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With the health that Pompey gives him, else he is a\nvery epicure.","sourceLine":"2.7.55","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to MENAS] Go hang, sir, hang! Tell me of\nthat? away!\nDo as I bid you. Where's this cup I call'd for?","sourceLine":"2.7.57","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to POMPEY] If for the sake of merit thou\nwilt hear me,\nRise from thy stool.","sourceLine":"2.7.60","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to MENAS] I think thou'rt mad.\nThe matter?","sourceLine":"2.7.63","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Rises, and walks aside","id":"cleopatra:2.7:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have ever held my cap off to thy fortunes.","sourceLine":"2.7.65","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hast served me with much faith. What's else to say?\nBe jolly, lords.","sourceLine":"2.7.66","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These quick-sands, Lepidus,\nKeep off them, for you sink.","sourceLine":"2.7.68","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wilt thou be lord of all the world?","sourceLine":"2.7.70","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say'st thou?","sourceLine":"2.7.71","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wilt thou be lord of the whole world? That's twice.","sourceLine":"2.7.72","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How should that be?","sourceLine":"2.7.73","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But entertain it,\nAnd, though thou think me poor, I am the man\nWill give thee all the world.","sourceLine":"2.7.74","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hast thou drunk well?","sourceLine":"2.7.77","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, Pompey, I have kept me from the cup.\nThou art, if thou darest be, the earthly Jove:\nWhate'er the ocean pales, or sky inclips,\nIs thine, if thou wilt ha't.","sourceLine":"2.7.78","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Show me which way.","sourceLine":"2.7.82","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These three world-sharers, these competitors,\nAre in thy vessel: let me cut the cable;\nAnd, when we are put off, fall to their throats:\nAll there is thine.","sourceLine":"2.7.83","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, this thou shouldst have done,\nAnd not have spoke on't! In me 'tis villany;\nIn thee't had been good service. Thou must know,\n'Tis not my profit that does lead mine honour;\nMine honour, it. Repent that e'er thy tongue\nHath so betray'd thine act: being done unknown,\nI should have found it afterwards well done;\nBut must condemn it now. Desist, and drink.","sourceLine":"2.7.87","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] For this,\nI'll never follow thy pall'd fortunes more.\nWho seeks, and will not take when once 'tis offer'd,\nShall never find it more.","sourceLine":"2.7.95","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This health to Lepidus!","sourceLine":"2.7.99","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bear him ashore. I'll pledge it for him, Pompey.","sourceLine":"2.7.100","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here's to thee, Menas!","sourceLine":"2.7.101","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Enobarbus, welcome!","sourceLine":"2.7.102","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fill till the cup be hid.","sourceLine":"2.7.103","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's a strong fellow, Menas.","sourceLine":"2.7.104","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:54"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Pointing to the Attendant who carries off LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why?","sourceLine":"2.7.105","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A' bears the third part of the world, man; see'st\nnot?","sourceLine":"2.7.106","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The third part, then, is drunk: would it were all,\nThat it might go on wheels!","sourceLine":"2.7.108","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Drink thou; increase the reels.","sourceLine":"2.7.110","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come.","sourceLine":"2.7.111","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is not yet an Alexandrian feast.","sourceLine":"2.7.112","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It ripens towards it. Strike the vessels, ho?\nHere is to Caesar!","sourceLine":"2.7.113","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I could well forbear't.\nIt's monstrous labour, when I wash my brain,\nAnd it grows fouler.","sourceLine":"2.7.115","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.7:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be a child o' the time.","sourceLine":"2.7.118","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Possess it, I'll make answer:\nBut I had rather fast from all four days\nThan drink so much in one.","sourceLine":"2.7.119","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.7:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ha, my brave emperor!","sourceLine":"2.7.122","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:66"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall we dance now the Egyptian Bacchanals,\nAnd celebrate our drink?","sourceLine":"2.7.123","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let's ha't, good soldier.","sourceLine":"2.7.125","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, let's all take hands,\nTill that the conquering wine hath steep'd our sense\nIn soft and delicate Lethe.","sourceLine":"2.7.126","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All take hands.\nMake battery to our ears with the loud music:\nThe while I'll place you: then the boy shall sing;\nThe holding every man shall bear as loud\nAs his strong sides can volley.","sourceLine":"2.7.129","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Music plays. DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS places them hand in hand","id":"cleopatra:2.7:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"THE SONG.\nCome, thou monarch of the vine,\nPlumpy Bacchus with pink eyne!\nIn thy fats our cares be drown'd,\nWith thy grapes our hairs be crown'd:\nCup us, till the world go round,\nCup us, till the world go round!","sourceLine":"2.7.134","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What would you more? Pompey, good night. Good brother,\nLet me request you off: our graver business\nFrowns at this levity. Gentle lords, let's part;\nYou see we have burnt our cheeks: strong Enobarb\nIs weaker than the wine; and mine own tongue\nSplits what it speaks: the wild disguise hath almost\nAntick'd us all. What needs more words? Good night.\nGood Antony, your hand.","sourceLine":"2.7.141","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:2.7:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll try you on the shore.","sourceLine":"2.7.149","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And shall, sir; give's your hand.","sourceLine":"2.7.150","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Antony,\nYou have my father's house,--But, what? we are friends.\nCome, down into the boat.","sourceLine":"2.7.151","speaker":"POMPEY","id":"cleopatra:2.7:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take heed you fall not.","sourceLine":"2.7.154","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:78"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS and MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Menas, I'll not on shore.","sourceLine":"2.7.155","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, to my cabin.\nThese drums! these trumpets, flutes! what!\nLet Neptune hear we bid a loud farewell\nTo these great fellows: sound and be hang'd, sound out!","sourceLine":"2.7.156","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:81"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sound a flourish, with drums","id":"cleopatra:2.7:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ho! says a' There's my cap.","sourceLine":"2.7.160","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ho! Noble captain, come.","sourceLine":"2.7.161","speaker":"MENAS","id":"cleopatra:2.7:84"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:2.7:85"}],"scene":7},{"id":"3.1","label":"Act 3 · SCENE I. A plain in Syria.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter VENTIDIUS as it were in triumph, with SILIUS, and other Romans, Officers, and Soldiers; the dead body of PACORUS borne before him","id":"cleopatra:3.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, darting Parthia, art thou struck; and now\nPleased fortune does of Marcus Crassus' death\nMake me revenger. Bear the king's son's body\nBefore our army. Thy Pacorus, Orodes,\nPays this for Marcus Crassus.","sourceLine":"3.1.1","speaker":"VENTIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Noble Ventidius,\nWhilst yet with Parthian blood thy sword is warm,\nThe fugitive Parthians follow; spur through Media,\nMesopotamia, and the shelters whither\nThe routed fly: so thy grand captain Antony\nShall set thee on triumphant chariots and\nPut garlands on thy head.","sourceLine":"3.1.6","speaker":"SILIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Silius, Silius,\nI have done enough; a lower place, note well,\nMay make too great an act: for learn this, Silius;\nBetter to leave undone, than by our deed\nAcquire too high a fame when him we serve's away.\nCaesar and Antony have ever won\nMore in their officer than person: Sossius,\nOne of my place in Syria, his lieutenant,\nFor quick accumulation of renown,\nWhich he achieved by the minute, lost his favour.\nWho does i' the wars more than his captain can\nBecomes his captain's captain: and ambition,\nThe soldier's virtue, rather makes choice of loss,\nThan gain which darkens him.\nI could do more to do Antonius good,\nBut 'twould offend him; and in his offence\nShould my performance perish.","sourceLine":"3.1.13","speaker":"VENTIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hast, Ventidius,\nthat\nWithout the which a soldier, and his sword,\nGrants scarce distinction. Thou wilt write to Antony!","sourceLine":"3.1.30","speaker":"SILIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll humbly signify what in his name,\nThat magical word of war, we have effected;\nHow, with his banners and his well-paid ranks,\nThe ne'er-yet-beaten horse of Parthia\nWe have jaded out o' the field.","sourceLine":"3.1.34","speaker":"VENTIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is he now?","sourceLine":"3.1.39","speaker":"SILIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He purposeth to Athens: whither, with what haste\nThe weight we must convey with's will permit,\nWe shall appear before him. On there; pass along!","sourceLine":"3.1.40","speaker":"VENTIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.1:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.1:9"}],"scene":1},{"id":"3.2","label":"Act 3 · SCENE II. Rome. An ante-chamber in OCTAVIUS CAESAR's house.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter AGRIPPA at one door, DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS at another","id":"cleopatra:3.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, are the brothers parted?","sourceLine":"3.2.1","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They have dispatch'd with Pompey, he is gone;\nThe other three are sealing. Octavia weeps\nTo part from Rome; Caesar is sad; and Lepidus,\nSince Pompey's feast, as Menas says, is troubled\nWith the green sickness.","sourceLine":"3.2.2","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis a noble Lepidus.","sourceLine":"3.2.7","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A very fine one: O, how he loves Caesar!","sourceLine":"3.2.8","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, but how dearly he adores Mark Antony!","sourceLine":"3.2.9","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar? Why, he's the Jupiter of men.","sourceLine":"3.2.10","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's Antony? The god of Jupiter.","sourceLine":"3.2.11","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Spake you of Caesar? How! the non-pareil!","sourceLine":"3.2.12","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Antony! O thou Arabian bird!","sourceLine":"3.2.13","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would you praise Caesar, say 'Caesar:' go no further.","sourceLine":"3.2.14","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed, he plied them both with excellent praises.","sourceLine":"3.2.15","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But he loves Caesar best; yet he loves Antony:\nHo! hearts, tongues, figures, scribes, bards,\npoets, cannot\nThink, speak, cast, write, sing, number, ho!\nHis love to Antony. But as for Caesar,\nKneel down, kneel down, and wonder.","sourceLine":"3.2.16","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Both he loves.","sourceLine":"3.2.22","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are his shards, and he their beetle.","sourceLine":"3.2.23","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Trumpets within","id":"cleopatra:3.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So;\nThis is to horse. Adieu, noble Agrippa.","sourceLine":"3.2.24","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good fortune, worthy soldier; and farewell.","sourceLine":"3.2.26","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, MARK ANTONY, LEPIDUS, and OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No further, sir.","sourceLine":"3.2.27","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You take from me a great part of myself;\nUse me well in 't. Sister, prove such a wife\nAs my thoughts make thee, and as my farthest band\nShall pass on thy approof. Most noble Antony,\nLet not the piece of virtue, which is set\nBetwixt us as the cement of our love,\nTo keep it builded, be the ram to batter\nThe fortress of it; for better might we\nHave loved without this mean, if on both parts\nThis be not cherish'd.","sourceLine":"3.2.28","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make me not offended\nIn your distrust.","sourceLine":"3.2.38","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have said.","sourceLine":"3.2.40","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall not find,\nThough you be therein curious, the least cause\nFor what you seem to fear: so, the gods keep you,\nAnd make the hearts of Romans serve your ends!\nWe will here part.","sourceLine":"3.2.41","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, my dearest sister, fare thee well:\nThe elements be kind to thee, and make\nThy spirits all of comfort! fare thee well.","sourceLine":"3.2.46","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My noble brother!","sourceLine":"3.2.49","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The April 's in her eyes: it is love's spring,\nAnd these the showers to bring it on. Be cheerful.","sourceLine":"3.2.50","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, look well to my husband's house; and--","sourceLine":"3.2.52","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, Octavia?","sourceLine":"3.2.53","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll tell you in your ear.","sourceLine":"3.2.54","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Her tongue will not obey her heart, nor can\nHer heart inform her tongue,--the swan's\ndown-feather,\nThat stands upon the swell at full of tide,\nAnd neither way inclines.","sourceLine":"3.2.55","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to AGRIPPA] Will Caesar weep?","sourceLine":"3.2.60","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS] He has a cloud in 's face.","sourceLine":"3.2.61","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to AGRIPPA] He were the worse for that,\nwere he a horse;\nSo is he, being a man.","sourceLine":"3.2.62","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS] Why, Enobarbus,\nWhen Antony found Julius Caesar dead,\nHe cried almost to roaring; and he wept\nWhen at Philippi he found Brutus slain.","sourceLine":"3.2.65","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to AGRIPPA] That year, indeed, he was\ntroubled with a rheum;\nWhat willingly he did confound he wail'd,\nBelieve't, till I wept too.","sourceLine":"3.2.69","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, sweet Octavia,\nYou shall hear from me still; the time shall not\nOut-go my thinking on you.","sourceLine":"3.2.73","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, sir, come;\nI'll wrestle with you in my strength of love:\nLook, here I have you; thus I let you go,\nAnd give you to the gods.","sourceLine":"3.2.76","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Adieu; be happy!","sourceLine":"3.2.80","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let all the number of the stars give light\nTo thy fair way!","sourceLine":"3.2.81","speaker":"LEPIDUS","id":"cleopatra:3.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, fa rewell!","sourceLine":"3.2.83","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.2:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Kisses OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell!","sourceLine":"3.2.84","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.2:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Trumpets sound. Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.2:44"}],"scene":2},{"id":"3.3","label":"Act 3 · SCENE III. Alexandria. CLEOPATRA's palace.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, CHARMIAN, IRAS, and ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:3.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is the fellow?","sourceLine":"3.3.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Half afeard to come.","sourceLine":"3.3.2","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:3.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go to, go to.","sourceLine":"3.3.3","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Messenger as before","id":"cleopatra:3.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come hither, sir.","sourceLine":"3.3.4","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good majesty,\nHerod of Jewry dare not look upon you\nBut when you are well pleased.","sourceLine":"3.3.5","speaker":"ALEXAS","id":"cleopatra:3.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That Herod's head\nI'll have: but how, when Antony is gone\nThrough whom I might command it? Come thou near.","sourceLine":"3.3.8","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most gracious majesty,--","sourceLine":"3.3.11","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Didst thou behold Octavia?","sourceLine":"3.3.12","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, dread queen.","sourceLine":"3.3.13","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where?","sourceLine":"3.3.14","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, in Rome;\nI look'd her in the face, and saw her led\nBetween her brother and Mark Antony.","sourceLine":"3.3.15","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is she as tall as me?","sourceLine":"3.3.18","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is not, madam.","sourceLine":"3.3.19","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Didst hear her speak? is she shrill-tongued or low?","sourceLine":"3.3.20","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I heard her speak; she is low-voiced.","sourceLine":"3.3.21","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's not so good: he cannot like her long.","sourceLine":"3.3.22","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Like her! O Isis! 'tis impossible.","sourceLine":"3.3.23","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think so, Charmian: dull of tongue, and dwarfish!\nWhat majesty is in her gait? Remember,\nIf e'er thou look'dst on majesty.","sourceLine":"3.3.24","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She creeps:\nHer motion and her station are as one;\nShe shows a body rather than a life,\nA statue than a breather.","sourceLine":"3.3.27","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is this certain?","sourceLine":"3.3.31","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or I have no observance.","sourceLine":"3.3.32","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Three in Egypt\nCannot make better note.","sourceLine":"3.3.33","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He's very knowing;\nI do perceive't: there's nothing in her yet:\nThe fellow has good judgment.","sourceLine":"3.3.35","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Excellent.","sourceLine":"3.3.38","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Guess at her years, I prithee.","sourceLine":"3.3.39","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam,\nShe was a widow,--","sourceLine":"3.3.40","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Widow! Charmian, hark.","sourceLine":"3.3.42","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I do think she's thirty.","sourceLine":"3.3.43","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bear'st thou her face in mind? is't long or round?","sourceLine":"3.3.44","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Round even to faultiness.","sourceLine":"3.3.45","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For the most part, too, they are foolish that are so.\nHer hair, what colour?","sourceLine":"3.3.46","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Brown, madam: and her forehead\nAs low as she would wish it.","sourceLine":"3.3.48","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's gold for thee.\nThou must not take my former sharpness ill:\nI will employ thee back again; I find thee\nMost fit for business: go make thee ready;\nOur letters are prepared.","sourceLine":"3.3.50","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A proper man.","sourceLine":"3.3.55","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed, he is so: I repent me much\nThat so I harried him. Why, methinks, by him,\nThis creature's no such thing.","sourceLine":"3.3.56","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing, madam.","sourceLine":"3.3.59","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The man hath seen some majesty, and should know.","sourceLine":"3.3.60","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hath he seen majesty? Isis else defend,\nAnd serving you so long!","sourceLine":"3.3.61","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have one thing more to ask him yet, good Charmian:\nBut 'tis no matter; thou shalt bring him to me\nWhere I will write. All may be well enough.","sourceLine":"3.3.63","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I warrant you, madam.","sourceLine":"3.3.66","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.3:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.3:44"}],"scene":3},{"id":"3.4","label":"Act 3 · SCENE IV. Athens. A room in MARK ANTONY's house.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, nay, Octavia, not only that,--\nThat were excusable, that, and thousands more\nOf semblable import,--but he hath waged\nNew wars 'gainst Pompey; made his will, and read it\nTo public ear:\nSpoke scantly of me: when perforce he could not\nBut pay me terms of honour, cold and sickly\nHe vented them; most narrow measure lent me:\nWhen the best hint was given him, he not took't,\nOr did it from his teeth.","sourceLine":"3.4.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O my good lord,\nBelieve not all; or, if you must believe,\nStomach not all. A more unhappy lady,\nIf this division chance, ne'er stood between,\nPraying for both parts:\nThe good gods me presently,\nWhen I shall pray, 'O bless my lord and husband!'\nUndo that prayer, by crying out as loud,\n'O, bless my brother!' Husband win, win brother,\nPrays, and destroys the prayer; no midway\n'Twixt these extremes at all.","sourceLine":"3.4.11","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gentle Octavia,\nLet your best love draw to that point, which seeks\nBest to preserve it: if I lose mine honour,\nI lose myself: better I were not yours\nThan yours so branchless. But, as you requested,\nYourself shall go between 's: the mean time, lady,\nI'll raise the preparation of a war\nShall stain your brother: make your soonest haste;\nSo your desires are yours.","sourceLine":"3.4.22","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thanks to my lord.\nThe Jove of power make me most weak, most weak,\nYour reconciler! Wars 'twixt you twain would be\nAs if the world should cleave, and that slain men\nShould solder up the rift.","sourceLine":"3.4.31","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When it appears to you where this begins,\nTurn your displeasure that way: for our faults\nCan never be so equal, that your love\nCan equally move with them. Provide your going;\nChoose your own company, and command what cost\nYour heart has mind to.","sourceLine":"3.4.36","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.4:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.4:7"}],"scene":4},{"id":"3.5","label":"Act 3 · SCENE V. The same. Another room.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS and EROS, meeting","id":"cleopatra:3.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, friend Eros!","sourceLine":"3.5.1","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's strange news come, sir.","sourceLine":"3.5.2","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, man?","sourceLine":"3.5.3","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar and Lepidus have made wars upon Pompey.","sourceLine":"3.5.4","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is old: what is the success?","sourceLine":"3.5.5","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, having made use of him in the wars 'gainst\nPompey, presently denied him rivality; would not let\nhim partake in the glory of the action: and not\nresting here, accuses him of letters he had formerly\nwrote to Pompey; upon his own appeal, seizes him: so\nthe poor third is up, till death enlarge his confine.","sourceLine":"3.5.6","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, world, thou hast a pair of chaps, no more;\nAnd throw between them all the food thou hast,\nThey'll grind the one the other. Where's Antony?","sourceLine":"3.5.12","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He's walking in the garden--thus; and spurns\nThe rush that lies before him; cries, 'Fool Lepidus!'\nAnd threats the throat of that his officer\nThat murder'd Pompey.","sourceLine":"3.5.15","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our great navy's rigg'd.","sourceLine":"3.5.19","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For Italy and Caesar. More, Domitius;\nMy lord desires you presently: my news\nI might have told hereafter.","sourceLine":"3.5.20","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Twill be naught:\nBut let it be. Bring me to Antony.","sourceLine":"3.5.23","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, sir.","sourceLine":"3.5.25","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.5:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.5:14"}],"scene":5},{"id":"3.6","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VI. Rome. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's house.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, AGRIPPA, and MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:3.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Contemning Rome, he has done all this, and more,\nIn Alexandria: here's the manner of 't:\nI' the market-place, on a tribunal silver'd,\nCleopatra and himself in chairs of gold\nWere publicly enthroned: at the feet sat\nCaesarion, whom they call my father's son,\nAnd all the unlawful issue that their lust\nSince then hath made between them. Unto her\nHe gave the stablishment of Egypt; made her\nOf lower Syria, Cyprus, Lydia,\nAbsolute queen.","sourceLine":"3.6.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This in the public eye?","sourceLine":"3.6.12","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:3.6:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I' the common show-place, where they exercise.\nHis sons he there proclaim'd the kings of kings:\nGreat Media, Parthia, and Armenia.\nHe gave to Alexander; to Ptolemy he assign'd\nSyria, Cilicia, and Phoenicia: she\nIn the habiliments of the goddess Isis\nThat day appear'd; and oft before gave audience,\nAs 'tis reported, so.","sourceLine":"3.6.13","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let Rome be thus Inform'd.","sourceLine":"3.6.21","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:3.6:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who, queasy with his insolence\nAlready, will their good thoughts call from him.","sourceLine":"3.6.22","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The people know it; and have now received\nHis accusations.","sourceLine":"3.6.24","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who does he accuse?","sourceLine":"3.6.26","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar: and that, having in Sicily\nSextus Pompeius spoil'd, we had not rated him\nHis part o' the isle: then does he say, he lent me\nSome shipping unrestored: lastly, he frets\nThat Lepidus of the triumvirate\nShould be deposed; and, being, that we detain\nAll his revenue.","sourceLine":"3.6.27","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, this should be answer'd.","sourceLine":"3.6.34","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis done already, and the messenger gone.\nI have told him, Lepidus was grown too cruel;\nThat he his high authority abused,\nAnd did deserve his change: for what I have conquer'd,\nI grant him part; but then, in his Armenia,\nAnd other of his conquer'd kingdoms, I\nDemand the like.","sourceLine":"3.6.35","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He'll never yield to that.","sourceLine":"3.6.42","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:3.6:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor must not then be yielded to in this.","sourceLine":"3.6.43","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIA with her train","id":"cleopatra:3.6:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hail, Caesar, and my lord! hail, most dear Caesar!","sourceLine":"3.6.44","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That ever I should call thee castaway!","sourceLine":"3.6.45","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have not call'd me so, nor have you cause.","sourceLine":"3.6.46","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why have you stol'n upon us thus! You come not\nLike Caesar's sister: the wife of Antony\nShould have an army for an usher, and\nThe neighs of horse to tell of her approach\nLong ere she did appear; the trees by the way\nShould have borne men; and expectation fainted,\nLonging for what it had not; nay, the dust\nShould have ascended to the roof of heaven,\nRaised by your populous troops: but you are come\nA market-maid to Rome; and have prevented\nThe ostentation of our love, which, left unshown,\nIs often left unloved; we should have met you\nBy sea and land; supplying every stage\nWith an augmented greeting.","sourceLine":"3.6.47","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good my lord,\nTo come thus was I not constrain'd, but did\nOn my free will. My lord, Mark Antony,\nHearing that you prepared for war, acquainted\nMy grieved ear withal; whereon, I begg'd\nHis pardon for return.","sourceLine":"3.6.61","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which soon he granted,\nBeing an obstruct 'tween his lust and him.","sourceLine":"3.6.67","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do not say so, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.6.69","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have eyes upon him,\nAnd his affairs come to me on the wind.\nWhere is he now?","sourceLine":"3.6.70","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, in Athens.","sourceLine":"3.6.73","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my most wronged sister; Cleopatra\nHath nodded him to her. He hath given his empire\nUp to a whore; who now are levying\nThe kings o' the earth for war; he hath assembled\nBocchus, the king of Libya; Archelaus,\nOf Cappadocia; Philadelphos, king\nOf Paphlagonia; the Thracian king, Adallas;\nKing Malchus of Arabia; King of Pont;\nHerod of Jewry; Mithridates, king\nOf Comagene; Polemon and Amyntas,\nThe kings of Mede and Lycaonia,\nWith a more larger list of sceptres.","sourceLine":"3.6.74","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay me, most wretched,\nThat have my heart parted betwixt two friends\nThat do afflict each other!","sourceLine":"3.6.86","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome hither:\nYour letters did withhold our breaking forth;\nTill we perceived, both how you were wrong led,\nAnd we in negligent danger. Cheer your heart;\nBe you not troubled with the time, which drives\nO'er your content these strong necessities;\nBut let determined things to destiny\nHold unbewail'd their way. Welcome to Rome;\nNothing more dear to me. You are abused\nBeyond the mark of thought: and the high gods,\nTo do you justice, make them ministers\nOf us and those that love you. Best of comfort;\nAnd ever welcome to us.","sourceLine":"3.6.89","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome, lady.","sourceLine":"3.6.102","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome, dear madam.\nEach heart in Rome does love and pity you:\nOnly the adulterous Antony, most large\nIn his abominations, turns you off;\nAnd gives his potent regiment to a trull,\nThat noises it against us.","sourceLine":"3.6.103","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:3.6:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it so, sir?","sourceLine":"3.6.109","speaker":"OCTAVIA","id":"cleopatra:3.6:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most certain. Sister, welcome: pray you,\nBe ever known to patience: my dear'st sister!","sourceLine":"3.6.110","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.6:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.6:31"}],"scene":6},{"id":"3.7","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VII. Near Actium. MARK ANTONY's camp.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA and DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will be even with thee, doubt it not.","sourceLine":"3.7.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But why, why, why?","sourceLine":"3.7.2","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hast forspoke my being in these wars,\nAnd say'st it is not fit.","sourceLine":"3.7.3","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, is it, is it?","sourceLine":"3.7.5","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If not denounced against us, why should not we\nBe there in person?","sourceLine":"3.7.6","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Well, I could reply:\nIf we should serve with horse and mares together,\nThe horse were merely lost; the mares would bear\nA soldier and his horse.","sourceLine":"3.7.8","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is't you say?","sourceLine":"3.7.12","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your presence needs must puzzle Antony;\nTake from his heart, take from his brain,\nfrom's time,\nWhat should not then be spared. He is already\nTraduced for levity; and 'tis said in Rome\nThat Photinus an eunuch and your maids\nManage this war.","sourceLine":"3.7.13","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sink Rome, and their tongues rot\nThat speak against us! A charge we bear i' the war,\nAnd, as the president of my kingdom, will\nAppear there for a man. Speak not against it:\nI will not stay behind.","sourceLine":"3.7.20","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, I have done.\nHere comes the emperor.","sourceLine":"3.7.25","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it not strange, Canidius,\nThat from Tarentum and Brundusium\nHe could so quickly cut the Ionian sea,\nAnd take in Toryne? You have heard on't, sweet?","sourceLine":"3.7.27","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Celerity is never more admired\nThan by the negligent.","sourceLine":"3.7.31","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A good rebuke,\nWhich might have well becomed the best of men,\nTo taunt at slackness. Canidius, we\nWill fight with him by sea.","sourceLine":"3.7.33","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By sea! what else?","sourceLine":"3.7.37","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why will my lord do so?","sourceLine":"3.7.38","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For that he dares us to't.","sourceLine":"3.7.39","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So hath my lord dared him to single fight.","sourceLine":"3.7.40","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, and to wage this battle at Pharsalia.\nWhere Caesar fought with Pompey: but these offers,\nWhich serve not for his vantage, be shakes off;\nAnd so should you.","sourceLine":"3.7.41","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your ships are not well mann'd;\nYour mariners are muleters, reapers, people\nIngross'd by swift impress; in Caesar's fleet\nAre those that often have 'gainst Pompey fought:\nTheir ships are yare; yours, heavy: no disgrace\nShall fall you for refusing him at sea,\nBeing prepared for land.","sourceLine":"3.7.45","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By sea, by sea.","sourceLine":"3.7.52","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most worthy sir, you therein throw away\nThe absolute soldiership you have by land;\nDistract your army, which doth most consist\nOf war-mark'd footmen; leave unexecuted\nYour own renowned knowledge; quite forego\nThe way which promises assurance; and\nGive up yourself merely to chance and hazard,\nFrom firm security.","sourceLine":"3.7.53","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll fight at sea.","sourceLine":"3.7.61","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have sixty sails, Caesar none better.","sourceLine":"3.7.62","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.7:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our overplus of shipping will we burn;\nAnd, with the rest full-mann'd, from the head of Actium\nBeat the approaching Caesar. But if we fail,\nWe then can do't at land.","sourceLine":"3.7.63","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.7:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy business?","sourceLine":"3.7.67","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The news is true, my lord; he is descried;\nCaesar has taken Toryne.","sourceLine":"3.7.68","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.7:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Can he be there in person? 'tis impossible;\nStrange that power should be. Canidius,\nOur nineteen legions thou shalt hold by land,\nAnd our twelve thousand horse. We'll to our ship:\nAway, my Thetis!","sourceLine":"3.7.70","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Soldier","id":"cleopatra:3.7:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, worthy soldier?","sourceLine":"3.7.75","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O noble emperor, do not fight by sea;\nTrust not to rotten planks: do you misdoubt\nThis sword and these my wounds? Let the Egyptians\nAnd the Phoenicians go a-ducking; we\nHave used to conquer, standing on the earth,\nAnd fighting foot to foot.","sourceLine":"3.7.76","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:3.7:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, well: away!","sourceLine":"3.7.82","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.7:34"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt MARK ANTONY, QUEEN CLEOPATRA, and DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By Hercules, I think I am i' the right.","sourceLine":"3.7.83","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:3.7:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Soldier, thou art: but his whole action grows\nNot in the power on't: so our leader's led,\nAnd we are women's men.","sourceLine":"3.7.84","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You keep by land\nThe legions and the horse whole, do you not?","sourceLine":"3.7.87","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:3.7:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marcus Octavius, Marcus Justeius,\nPublicola, and Caelius, are for sea:\nBut we keep whole by land. This speed of Caesar's\nCarries beyond belief.","sourceLine":"3.7.89","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"While he was yet in Rome,\nHis power went out in such distractions as\nBeguiled all spies.","sourceLine":"3.7.93","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:3.7:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who's his lieutenant, hear you?","sourceLine":"3.7.96","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They say, one Taurus.","sourceLine":"3.7.97","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:3.7:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well I know the man.","sourceLine":"3.7.98","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.7:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The emperor calls Canidius.","sourceLine":"3.7.99","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:3.7:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With news the time's with labour, and throes forth,\nEach minute, some.","sourceLine":"3.7.100","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.7:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.7:47"}],"scene":7},{"id":"3.8","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VIII. A plain near Actium.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, and TAURUS, with his army, marching","id":"cleopatra:3.8:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Taurus!","sourceLine":"3.8.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.8:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord?","sourceLine":"3.8.2","speaker":"TAURUS","id":"cleopatra:3.8:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Strike not by land; keep whole: provoke not battle,\nTill we have done at sea. Do not exceed\nThe prescript of this scroll: our fortune lies\nUpon this jump.","sourceLine":"3.8.3","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.8:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.8:5"}],"scene":8},{"id":"3.9","label":"Act 3 · SCENE IX. Another part of the plain.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.9:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Set we our squadrons on yond side o' the hill,\nIn eye of Caesar's battle; from which place\nWe may the number of the ships behold,\nAnd so proceed accordingly.","sourceLine":"3.9.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.9:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.9:3"}],"scene":9},{"id":"3.10","label":"Act 3 · SCENE X. Another part of the plain.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"CANIDIUS marcheth with his land army one way over the stage; and TAURUS, the lieutenant of OCTAVIUS CAESAR, the other way. After their going in, is heard the noise of a sea-fight","id":"cleopatra:3.10:1"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum. Enter DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Naught, naught all, naught! I can behold no longer:\nThe Antoniad, the Egyptian admiral,\nWith all their sixty, fly and turn the rudder:\nTo see't mine eyes are blasted.","sourceLine":"3.10.1","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gods and goddesses,\nAll the whole synod of them!","sourceLine":"3.10.5","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's thy passion!","sourceLine":"3.10.7","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The greater cantle of the world is lost\nWith very ignorance; we have kiss'd away\nKingdoms and provinces.","sourceLine":"3.10.8","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How appears the fight?","sourceLine":"3.10.11","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"On our side like the token'd pestilence,\nWhere death is sure. Yon ribaudred nag of Egypt,--\nWhom leprosy o'ertake!--i' the midst o' the fight,\nWhen vantage like a pair of twins appear'd,\nBoth as the same, or rather ours the elder,\nThe breese upon her, like a cow in June,\nHoists sails and flies.","sourceLine":"3.10.12","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That I beheld:\nMine eyes did sicken at the sight, and could not\nEndure a further view.","sourceLine":"3.10.19","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She once being loof'd,\nThe noble ruin of her magic, Antony,\nClaps on his sea-wing, and, like a doting mallard,\nLeaving the fight in height, flies after her:\nI never saw an action of such shame;\nExperience, manhood, honour, ne'er before\nDid violate so itself.","sourceLine":"3.10.22","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alack, alack!","sourceLine":"3.10.29","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our fortune on the sea is out of breath,\nAnd sinks most lamentably. Had our general\nBeen what he knew himself, it had gone well:\nO, he has given example for our flight,\nMost grossly, by his own!","sourceLine":"3.10.30","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, are you thereabouts?\nWhy, then, good night indeed.","sourceLine":"3.10.35","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Toward Peloponnesus are they fled.","sourceLine":"3.10.37","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis easy to't; and there I will attend\nWhat further comes.","sourceLine":"3.10.38","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To Caesar will I render\nMy legions and my horse: six kings already\nShow me the way of yielding.","sourceLine":"3.10.40","speaker":"CANIDIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll yet follow\nThe wounded chance of Antony, though my reason\nSits in the wind against me.","sourceLine":"3.10.43","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.10:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.10:20"}],"scene":10},{"id":"3.11","label":"Act 3 · SCENE XI. Alexandria. CLEOPATRA's palace.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY with Attendants","id":"cleopatra:3.11:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark! the land bids me tread no more upon't;\nIt is ashamed to bear me! Friends, come hither:\nI am so lated in the world, that I\nHave lost my way for ever: I have a ship\nLaden with gold; take that, divide it; fly,\nAnd make your peace with Caesar.","sourceLine":"3.11.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fly! not we.","sourceLine":"3.11.7","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:3.11:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have fled myself; and have instructed cowards\nTo run and show their shoulders. Friends, be gone;\nI have myself resolved upon a course\nWhich has no need of you; be gone:\nMy treasure's in the harbour, take it. O,\nI follow'd that I blush to look upon:\nMy very hairs do mutiny; for the white\nReprove the brown for rashness, and they them\nFor fear and doting. Friends, be gone: you shall\nHave letters from me to some friends that will\nSweep your way for you. Pray you, look not sad,\nNor make replies of loathness: take the hint\nWhich my despair proclaims; let that be left\nWhich leaves itself: to the sea-side straightway:\nI will possess you of that ship and treasure.\nLeave me, I pray, a little: pray you now:\nNay, do so; for, indeed, I have lost command,\nTherefore I pray you: I'll see you by and by.","sourceLine":"3.11.8","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Sits down","id":"cleopatra:3.11:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA led by CHARMIAN and IRAS; EROS following","id":"cleopatra:3.11:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, gentle madam, to him, comfort him.","sourceLine":"3.11.26","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do, most dear queen.","sourceLine":"3.11.27","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do! why: what else?","sourceLine":"3.11.28","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.11:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me sit down. O Juno!","sourceLine":"3.11.29","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.11:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no, no, no, no.","sourceLine":"3.11.30","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See you here, sir?","sourceLine":"3.11.31","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O fie, fie, fie!","sourceLine":"3.11.32","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam!","sourceLine":"3.11.33","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:3.11:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, O good empress!","sourceLine":"3.11.34","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, sir,--","sourceLine":"3.11.35","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, my lord, yes; he at Philippi kept\nHis sword e'en like a dancer; while I struck\nThe lean and wrinkled Cassius; and 'twas I\nThat the mad Brutus ended: he alone\nDealt on lieutenantry, and no practise had\nIn the brave squares of war: yet now--No matter.","sourceLine":"3.11.36","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, stand by.","sourceLine":"3.11.42","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.11:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The queen, my lord, the queen.","sourceLine":"3.11.43","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go to him, madam, speak to him:\nHe is unqualitied with very shame.","sourceLine":"3.11.44","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well then, sustain him: O!","sourceLine":"3.11.46","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.11:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most noble sir, arise; the queen approaches:\nHer head's declined, and death will seize her, but\nYour comfort makes the rescue.","sourceLine":"3.11.47","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have offended reputation,\nA most unnoble swerving.","sourceLine":"3.11.50","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, the queen.","sourceLine":"3.11.52","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:3.11:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, whither hast thou led me, Egypt? See,\nHow I convey my shame out of thine eyes\nBy looking back what I have left behind\n'Stroy'd in dishonour.","sourceLine":"3.11.53","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O my lord, my lord,\nForgive my fearful sails! I little thought\nYou would have follow'd.","sourceLine":"3.11.57","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.11:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Egypt, thou knew'st too well\nMy heart was to thy rudder tied by the strings,\nAnd thou shouldst tow me after: o'er my spirit\nThy full supremacy thou knew'st, and that\nThy beck might from the bidding of the gods\nCommand me.","sourceLine":"3.11.60","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, my pardon!","sourceLine":"3.11.66","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.11:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now I must\nTo the young man send humble treaties, dodge\nAnd palter in the shifts of lowness; who\nWith half the bulk o' the world play'd as I pleased,\nMaking and marring fortunes. You did know\nHow much you were my conqueror; and that\nMy sword, made weak by my affection, would\nObey it on all cause.","sourceLine":"3.11.67","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pardon, pardon!","sourceLine":"3.11.75","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.11:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fall not a tear, I say; one of them rates\nAll that is won and lost: give me a kiss;\nEven this repays me. We sent our schoolmaster;\nIs he come back? Love, I am full of lead.\nSome wine, within there, and our viands! Fortune knows\nWe scorn her most when most she offers blows.","sourceLine":"3.11.76","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.11:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.11:32"}],"scene":11},{"id":"3.12","label":"Act 3 · SCENE XII. Egypt. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's camp.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, DOLABELLA, THYREUS, with others","id":"cleopatra:3.12:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him appear that's come from Antony.\nKnow you him?","sourceLine":"3.12.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.12:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, 'tis his schoolmaster:\nAn argument that he is pluck'd, when hither\nHe sends so poor a pinion off his wing,\nWhich had superfluous kings for messengers\nNot many moons gone by.","sourceLine":"3.12.3","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:3.12:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter EUPHRONIUS, ambassador from MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.12:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Approach, and speak.","sourceLine":"3.12.8","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.12:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Such as I am, I come from Antony:\nI was of late as petty to his ends\nAs is the morn-dew on the myrtle-leaf\nTo his grand sea.","sourceLine":"3.12.9","speaker":"EUPHRONIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.12:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be't so: declare thine office.","sourceLine":"3.12.13","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.12:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord of his fortunes he salutes thee, and\nRequires to live in Egypt: which not granted,\nHe lessens his requests; and to thee sues\nTo let him breathe between the heavens and earth,\nA private man in Athens: this for him.\nNext, Cleopatra does confess thy greatness;\nSubmits her to thy might; and of thee craves\nThe circle of the Ptolemies for her heirs,\nNow hazarded to thy grace.","sourceLine":"3.12.14","speaker":"EUPHRONIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.12:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For Antony,\nI have no ears to his request. The queen\nOf audience nor desire shall fail, so she\nFrom Egypt drive her all-disgraced friend,\nOr take his life there: this if she perform,\nShe shall not sue unheard. So to them both.","sourceLine":"3.12.23","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.12:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fortune pursue thee!","sourceLine":"3.12.29","speaker":"EUPHRONIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.12:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bring him through the bands.","sourceLine":"3.12.30","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.12:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit EUPHRONIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.12:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.12:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From Antony win Cleopatra: promise,\nAnd in our name, what she requires; add more,\nFrom thine invention, offers: women are not\nIn their best fortunes strong; but want will perjure\nThe ne'er touch'd vestal: try thy cunning, Thyreus;\nMake thine own edict for thy pains, which we\nWill answer as a law.","sourceLine":"3.12.31","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.12:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, I go.","sourceLine":"3.12.38","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.12:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Observe how Antony becomes his flaw,\nAnd what thou think'st his very action speaks\nIn every power that moves.","sourceLine":"3.12.39","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:3.12:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, I shall.","sourceLine":"3.12.42","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.12:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:3.12:18"}],"scene":12},{"id":"3.13","label":"Act 3 · SCENE XIII. Alexandria. CLEOPATRA's palace.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS, CHARMIAN, and IRAS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What shall we do, Enobarbus?","sourceLine":"3.13.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Think, and die.","sourceLine":"3.13.2","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is Antony or we in fault for this?","sourceLine":"3.13.3","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Antony only, that would make his will\nLord of his reason. What though you fled\nFrom that great face of war, whose several ranges\nFrighted each other? why should he follow?\nThe itch of his affection should not then\nHave nick'd his captainship; at such a point,\nWhen half to half the world opposed, he being\nThe meered question: 'twas a shame no less\nThan was his loss, to course your flying flags,\nAnd leave his navy gazing.","sourceLine":"3.13.4","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Prithee, peace.","sourceLine":"3.13.14","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY with EUPHRONIUS, the Ambassador","id":"cleopatra:3.13:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is that his answer?","sourceLine":"3.13.15","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.13.16","speaker":"EUPHRONIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The queen shall then have courtesy, so she\nWill yield us up.","sourceLine":"3.13.17","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He says so.","sourceLine":"3.13.19","speaker":"EUPHRONIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let her know't.\nTo the boy Caesar send this grizzled head,\nAnd he will fill thy wishes to the brim\nWith principalities.","sourceLine":"3.13.20","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That head, my lord?","sourceLine":"3.13.24","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To him again: tell him he wears the rose\nOf youth upon him; from which the world should note\nSomething particular: his coin, ships, legions,\nMay be a coward's; whose ministers would prevail\nUnder the service of a child as soon\nAs i' the command of Caesar: I dare him therefore\nTo lay his gay comparisons apart,\nAnd answer me declined, sword against sword,\nOurselves alone. I'll write it: follow me.","sourceLine":"3.13.25","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt MARK ANTONY and EUPHRONIUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Yes, like enough, high-battled Caesar will\nUnstate his happiness, and be staged to the show,\nAgainst a sworder! I see men's judgments are\nA parcel of their fortunes; and things outward\nDo draw the inward quality after them,\nTo suffer all alike. That he should dream,\nKnowing all measures, the full Caesar will\nAnswer his emptiness! Caesar, thou hast subdued\nHis judgment too.","sourceLine":"3.13.34","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter an Attendant","id":"cleopatra:3.13:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A messenger from CAESAR.","sourceLine":"3.13.43","speaker":"Attendant","id":"cleopatra:3.13:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, no more ceremony? See, my women!\nAgainst the blown rose may they stop their nose\nThat kneel'd unto the buds. Admit him, sir.","sourceLine":"3.13.44","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Attendant","id":"cleopatra:3.13:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] Mine honesty and I begin to square.\nThe loyalty well held to fools does make\nOur faith mere folly: yet he that can endure\nTo follow with allegiance a fall'n lord\nDoes conquer him that did his master conquer\nAnd earns a place i' the story.","sourceLine":"3.13.47","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar's will?","sourceLine":"3.13.53","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear it apart.","sourceLine":"3.13.54","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"None but friends: say boldly.","sourceLine":"3.13.55","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, haply, are they friends to Antony.","sourceLine":"3.13.56","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He needs as many, sir, as Caesar has;\nOr needs not us. If Caesar please, our master\nWill leap to be his friend: for us, you know,\nWhose he is we are, and that is, Caesar's.","sourceLine":"3.13.57","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So.\nThus then, thou most renown'd: Caesar entreats,\nNot to consider in what case thou stand'st,\nFurther than he is Caesar.","sourceLine":"3.13.61","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go on: right royal.","sourceLine":"3.13.65","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He knows that you embrace not Antony\nAs you did love, but as you fear'd him.","sourceLine":"3.13.66","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O!","sourceLine":"3.13.68","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The scars upon your honour, therefore, he\nDoes pity, as constrained blemishes,\nNot as deserved.","sourceLine":"3.13.69","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is a god, and knows\nWhat is most right: mine honour was not yielded,\nBut conquer'd merely.","sourceLine":"3.13.72","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] To be sure of that,\nI will ask Antony. Sir, sir, thou art so leaky,\nThat we must leave thee to thy sinking, for\nThy dearest quit thee.","sourceLine":"3.13.75","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:34"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:3.13:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall I say to Caesar\nWhat you require of him? for he partly begs\nTo be desired to give. It much would please him,\nThat of his fortunes you should make a staff\nTo lean upon: but it would warm his spirits,\nTo hear from me you had left Antony,\nAnd put yourself under his shrowd,\nThe universal landlord.","sourceLine":"3.13.79","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's your name?","sourceLine":"3.13.87","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My name is Thyreus.","sourceLine":"3.13.88","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most kind messenger,\nSay to great Caesar this: in deputation\nI kiss his conquering hand: tell him, I am prompt\nTo lay my crown at 's feet, and there to kneel:\nTell him from his all-obeying breath I hear\nThe doom of Egypt.","sourceLine":"3.13.89","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis your noblest course.\nWisdom and fortune combating together,\nIf that the former dare but what it can,\nNo chance may shake it. Give me grace to lay\nMy duty on your hand.","sourceLine":"3.13.95","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your Caesar's father oft,\nWhen he hath mused of taking kingdoms in,\nBestow'd his lips on that unworthy place,\nAs it rain'd kisses.","sourceLine":"3.13.100","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter MARK ANTONY and DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Favours, by Jove that thunders!\nWhat art thou, fellow?","sourceLine":"3.13.104","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"One that but performs\nThe bidding of the fullest man, and worthiest\nTo have command obey'd.","sourceLine":"3.13.106","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] You will be whipp'd.","sourceLine":"3.13.109","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Approach, there! Ah, you kite! Now, gods\nand devils!\nAuthority melts from me: of late, when I cried 'Ho!'\nLike boys unto a muss, kings would start forth,\nAnd cry 'Your will?' Have you no ears? I am\nAntony yet.","sourceLine":"3.13.110","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Attendants","id":"cleopatra:3.13:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take hence this Jack, and whip him.","sourceLine":"3.13.116","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] 'Tis better playing with a lion's whelp\nThan with an old one dying.","sourceLine":"3.13.117","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Moon and stars!\nWhip him. Were't twenty of the greatest tributaries\nThat do acknowledge Caesar, should I find them\nSo saucy with the hand of she here,--what's her name,\nSince she was Cleopatra? Whip him, fellows,\nTill, like a boy, you see him cringe his face,\nAnd whine aloud for mercy: take him hence.","sourceLine":"3.13.119","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mark Antony!","sourceLine":"3.13.126","speaker":"THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tug him away: being whipp'd,\nBring him again: this Jack of Caesar's shall\nBear us an errand to him.","sourceLine":"3.13.127","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:52"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt Attendants with THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You were half blasted ere I knew you: ha!\nHave I my pillow left unpress'd in Rome,\nForborne the getting of a lawful race,\nAnd by a gem of women, to be abused\nBy one that looks on feeders?","sourceLine":"3.13.130","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good my lord,--","sourceLine":"3.13.135","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have been a boggler ever:\nBut when we in our viciousness grow hard--\nO misery on't!--the wise gods seel our eyes;\nIn our own filth drop our clear judgments; make us\nAdore our errors; laugh at's, while we strut\nTo our confusion.","sourceLine":"3.13.136","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, is't come to this?","sourceLine":"3.13.142","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I found you as a morsel cold upon\nDead Caesar's trencher; nay, you were a fragment\nOf Cneius Pompey's; besides what hotter hours,\nUnregister'd in vulgar fame, you have\nLuxuriously pick'd out: for, I am sure,\nThough you can guess what temperance should be,\nYou know not what it is.","sourceLine":"3.13.143","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wherefore is this?","sourceLine":"3.13.150","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To let a fellow that will take rewards\nAnd say 'God quit you!' be familiar with\nMy playfellow, your hand; this kingly seal\nAnd plighter of high hearts! O, that I were\nUpon the hill of Basan, to outroar\nThe horned herd! for I have savage cause;\nAnd to proclaim it civilly, were like\nA halter'd neck which does the hangman thank\nFor being yare about him.","sourceLine":"3.13.151","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:60"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter Attendants with THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is he whipp'd?","sourceLine":"3.13.160","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Soundly, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.13.161","speaker":"First Attendant","id":"cleopatra:3.13:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cried he? and begg'd a' pardon?","sourceLine":"3.13.162","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He did ask favour.","sourceLine":"3.13.163","speaker":"First Attendant","id":"cleopatra:3.13:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If that thy father live, let him repent\nThou wast not made his daughter; and be thou sorry\nTo follow Caesar in his triumph, since\nThou hast been whipp'd for following him: henceforth\nThe white hand of a lady fever thee,\nShake thou to look on 't. Get thee back to Caesar,\nTell him thy entertainment: look, thou say\nHe makes me angry with him; for he seems\nProud and disdainful, harping on what I am,\nNot what he knew I was: he makes me angry;\nAnd at this time most easy 'tis to do't,\nWhen my good stars, that were my former guides,\nHave empty left their orbs, and shot their fires\nInto the abysm of hell. If he mislike\nMy speech and what is done, tell him he has\nHipparchus, my enfranched bondman, whom\nHe may at pleasure whip, or hang, or torture,\nAs he shall like, to quit me: urge it thou:\nHence with thy stripes, begone!","sourceLine":"3.13.164","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:66"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit THYREUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have you done yet?","sourceLine":"3.13.183","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alack, our terrene moon\nIs now eclipsed; and it portends alone\nThe fall of Antony!","sourceLine":"3.13.184","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must stay his time.","sourceLine":"3.13.187","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To flatter Caesar, would you mingle eyes\nWith one that ties his points?","sourceLine":"3.13.188","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not know me yet?","sourceLine":"3.13.190","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cold-hearted toward me?","sourceLine":"3.13.191","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, dear, if I be so,\nFrom my cold heart let heaven engender hail,\nAnd poison it in the source; and the first stone\nDrop in my neck: as it determines, so\nDissolve my life! The next Caesarion smite!\nTill by degrees the memory of my womb,\nTogether with my brave Egyptians all,\nBy the discandying of this pelleted storm,\nLie graveless, till the flies and gnats of Nile\nHave buried them for prey!","sourceLine":"3.13.192","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am satisfied.\nCaesar sits down in Alexandria; where\nI will oppose his fate. Our force by land\nHath nobly held; our sever'd navy too\nHave knit again, and fleet, threatening most sea-like.\nWhere hast thou been, my heart? Dost thou hear, lady?\nIf from the field I shall return once more\nTo kiss these lips, I will appear in blood;\nI and my sword will earn our chronicle:\nThere's hope in't yet.","sourceLine":"3.13.202","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's my brave lord!","sourceLine":"3.13.212","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will be treble-sinew'd, hearted, breathed,\nAnd fight maliciously: for when mine hours\nWere nice and lucky, men did ransom lives\nOf me for jests; but now I'll set my teeth,\nAnd send to darkness all that stop me. Come,\nLet's have one other gaudy night: call to me\nAll my sad captains; fill our bowls once more;\nLet's mock the midnight bell.","sourceLine":"3.13.213","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is my birth-day:\nI had thought to have held it poor: but, since my lord\nIs Antony again, I will be Cleopatra.","sourceLine":"3.13.221","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We will yet do well.","sourceLine":"3.13.224","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Call all his noble captains to my lord.","sourceLine":"3.13.225","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:3.13:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do so, we'll speak to them; and to-night I'll force\nThe wine peep through their scars. Come on, my queen;\nThere's sap in't yet. The next time I do fight,\nI'll make death love me; for I will contend\nEven with his pestilent scythe.","sourceLine":"3.13.226","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:3.13:81"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now he'll outstare the lightning. To be furious,\nIs to be frighted out of fear; and in that mood\nThe dove will peck the estridge; and I see still,\nA diminution in our captain's brain\nRestores his heart: when valour preys on reason,\nIt eats the sword it fights with. I will seek\nSome way to leave him.","sourceLine":"3.13.231","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:3.13:83"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:3.13:84"}],"scene":13},{"id":"4.1","label":"Act 4 · SCENE I. Before Alexandria. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's camp.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, AGRIPPA, and MECAENAS, with his Army; OCTAVIUS CAESAR reading a letter","id":"cleopatra:4.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He calls me boy; and chides, as he had power\nTo beat me out of Egypt; my messenger\nHe hath whipp'd with rods; dares me to personal combat,\nCaesar to Antony: let the old ruffian know\nI have many other ways to die; meantime\nLaugh at his challenge.","sourceLine":"4.1.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:4.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar must think,\nWhen one so great begins to rage, he's hunted\nEven to falling. Give him no breath, but now\nMake boot of his distraction: never anger\nMade good guard for itself.","sourceLine":"4.1.7","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:4.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let our best heads\nKnow, that to-morrow the last of many battles\nWe mean to fight: within our files there are,\nOf those that served Mark Antony but late,\nEnough to fetch him in. See it done:\nAnd feast the army; we have store to do't,\nAnd they have earn'd the waste. Poor Antony!","sourceLine":"4.1.12","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:4.1:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.1:5"}],"scene":1},{"id":"4.2","label":"Act 4 · SCENE II. Alexandria. CLEOPATRA's palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY, CLEOPATRA, DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS, CHARMIAN, IRAS, ALEXAS, with others","id":"cleopatra:4.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He will not fight with me, Domitius.","sourceLine":"4.2.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","sourceLine":"4.2.2","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why should he not?","sourceLine":"4.2.3","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He thinks, being twenty times of better fortune,\nHe is twenty men to one.","sourceLine":"4.2.4","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To-morrow, soldier,\nBy sea and land I'll fight: or I will live,\nOr bathe my dying honour in the blood\nShall make it live again. Woo't thou fight well?","sourceLine":"4.2.6","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll strike, and cry 'Take all.'","sourceLine":"4.2.10","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well said; come on.\nCall forth my household servants: let's to-night\nBe bounteous at our meal.","sourceLine":"4.2.11","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter three or four Servitors","id":"cleopatra:4.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me thy hand,\nThou hast been rightly honest;--so hast thou;--\nThou,--and thou,--and thou:--you have served me well,\nAnd kings have been your fellows.","sourceLine":"4.2.14","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS] What means this?","sourceLine":"4.2.18","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to CLEOPATRA] 'Tis one of those odd\ntricks which sorrow shoots\nOut of the mind.","sourceLine":"4.2.19","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And thou art honest too.\nI wish I could be made so many men,\nAnd all of you clapp'd up together in\nAn Antony, that I might do you service\nSo good as you have done.","sourceLine":"4.2.22","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods forbid!","sourceLine":"4.2.27","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:4.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, my good fellows, wait on me to-night:\nScant not my cups; and make as much of me\nAs when mine empire was your fellow too,\nAnd suffer'd my command.","sourceLine":"4.2.28","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS] What does he mean?","sourceLine":"4.2.32","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to CLEOPATRA] To make his followers weep.","sourceLine":"4.2.33","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tend me to-night;\nMay be it is the period of your duty:\nHaply you shall not see me more; or if,\nA mangled shadow: perchance to-morrow\nYou'll serve another master. I look on you\nAs one that takes his leave. Mine honest friends,\nI turn you not away; but, like a master\nMarried to your good service, stay till death:\nTend me to-night two hours, I ask no more,\nAnd the gods yield you for't!","sourceLine":"4.2.34","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What mean you, sir,\nTo give them this discomfort? Look, they weep;\nAnd I, an ass, am onion-eyed: for shame,\nTransform us not to women.","sourceLine":"4.2.44","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ho, ho, ho!\nNow the witch take me, if I meant it thus!\nGrace grow where those drops fall!\nMy hearty friends,\nYou take me in too dolorous a sense;\nFor I spake to you for your comfort; did desire you\nTo burn this night with torches: know, my hearts,\nI hope well of to-morrow; and will lead you\nWhere rather I'll expect victorious life\nThan death and honour. Let's to supper, come,\nAnd drown consideration.","sourceLine":"4.2.48","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.2:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.2:21"}],"scene":2},{"id":"4.3","label":"Act 4 · SCENE III. The same. Before the palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter two Soldiers to their guard","id":"cleopatra:4.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Brother, good night: to-morrow is the day.","sourceLine":"4.3.1","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It will determine one way: fare you well.\nHeard you of nothing strange about the streets?","sourceLine":"4.3.2","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing. What news?","sourceLine":"4.3.4","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Belike 'tis but a rumour. Good night to you.","sourceLine":"4.3.5","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir, good night.","sourceLine":"4.3.6","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter two other Soldiers","id":"cleopatra:4.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Soldiers, have careful watch.","sourceLine":"4.3.7","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And you. Good night, good night.","sourceLine":"4.3.8","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They place themselves in every corner of the stage","id":"cleopatra:4.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here we: and if to-morrow\nOur navy thrive, I have an absolute hope\nOur landmen will stand up.","sourceLine":"4.3.9","speaker":"Fourth Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis a brave army,\nAnd full of purpose.","sourceLine":"4.3.12","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Music of the hautboys as under the stage","id":"cleopatra:4.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace! what noise?","sourceLine":"4.3.14","speaker":"Fourth Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"List, list!","sourceLine":"4.3.15","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark!","sourceLine":"4.3.16","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Music i' the air.","sourceLine":"4.3.17","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Under the earth.","sourceLine":"4.3.18","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It signs well, does it not?","sourceLine":"4.3.19","speaker":"Fourth Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","sourceLine":"4.3.20","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace, I say!\nWhat should this mean?","sourceLine":"4.3.21","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis the god Hercules, whom Antony loved,\nNow leaves him.","sourceLine":"4.3.23","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Walk; let's see if other watchmen\nDo hear what we do?","sourceLine":"4.3.25","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They advance to another post","id":"cleopatra:4.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, masters!","sourceLine":"4.3.27","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Speaking together] How now!\nHow now! do you hear this?","sourceLine":"4.3.28","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:4.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay; is't not strange?","sourceLine":"4.3.30","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you hear, masters? do you hear?","sourceLine":"4.3.31","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Follow the noise so far as we have quarter;\nLet's see how it will give off.","sourceLine":"4.3.32","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Content. 'Tis strange.","sourceLine":"4.3.34","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:4.3:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.3:31"}],"scene":3},{"id":"4.4","label":"Act 4 · SCENE IV. The same. A room in the palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and CLEOPATRA, CHARMIAN, and others attending","id":"cleopatra:4.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Eros! mine armour, Eros!","sourceLine":"4.4.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sleep a little.","sourceLine":"4.4.2","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my chuck. Eros, come; mine armour, Eros!","sourceLine":"4.4.3","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter EROS with armour","id":"cleopatra:4.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come good fellow, put mine iron on:\nIf fortune be not ours to-day, it is\nBecause we brave her: come.","sourceLine":"4.4.4","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, I'll help too.\nWhat's this for?","sourceLine":"4.4.7","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.4:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, let be, let be! thou art\nThe armourer of my heart: false, false; this, this.","sourceLine":"4.4.9","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sooth, la, I'll help: thus it must be.","sourceLine":"4.4.11","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, well;\nWe shall thrive now. Seest thou, my good fellow?\nGo put on thy defences.","sourceLine":"4.4.12","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Briefly, sir.","sourceLine":"4.4.15","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is not this buckled well?","sourceLine":"4.4.16","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rarely, rarely:\nHe that unbuckles this, till we do please\nTo daff't for our repose, shall hear a storm.\nThou fumblest, Eros; and my queen's a squire\nMore tight at this than thou: dispatch. O love,\nThat thou couldst see my wars to-day, and knew'st\nThe royal occupation! thou shouldst see\nA workman in't.","sourceLine":"4.4.17","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter an armed Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good morrow to thee; welcome:\nThou look'st like him that knows a warlike charge:\nTo business that we love we rise betime,\nAnd go to't with delight.","sourceLine":"4.4.25","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A thousand, sir,\nEarly though't be, have on their riveted trim,\nAnd at the port expect you.","sourceLine":"4.4.29","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.4:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Shout. Trumpets flourish","id":"cleopatra:4.4:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Captains and Soldiers","id":"cleopatra:4.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The morn is fair. Good morrow, general.","sourceLine":"4.4.32","speaker":"Captain","id":"cleopatra:4.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good morrow, general.","sourceLine":"4.4.33","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:4.4:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis well blown, lads:\nThis morning, like the spirit of a youth\nThat means to be of note, begins betimes.\nSo, so; come, give me that: this way; well said.\nFare thee well, dame, whate'er becomes of me:\nThis is a soldier's kiss: rebukeable","sourceLine":"4.4.34","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:21"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Kisses her","id":"cleopatra:4.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And worthy shameful cheque it were, to stand\nOn more mechanic compliment; I'll leave thee\nNow, like a man of steel. You that will fight,\nFollow me close; I'll bring you to't. Adieu.","sourceLine":"4.4.40","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.4:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt MARK ANTONY, EROS, Captains, and Soldiers","id":"cleopatra:4.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Please you, retire to your chamber.","sourceLine":"4.4.44","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lead me.\nHe goes forth gallantly. That he and Caesar might\nDetermine this great war in single fight!\nThen Antony,--but now--Well, on.","sourceLine":"4.4.45","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.4:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.4:27"}],"scene":4},{"id":"4.5","label":"Act 4 · SCENE V. Alexandria. MARK ANTONY's camp.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Trumpets sound. Enter MARK ANTONY and EROS; a Soldier meeting them","id":"cleopatra:4.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods make this a happy day to Antony!","sourceLine":"4.5.1","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would thou and those thy scars had once prevail'd\nTo make me fight at land!","sourceLine":"4.5.2","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hadst thou done so,\nThe kings that have revolted, and the soldier\nThat has this morning left thee, would have still\nFollow'd thy heels.","sourceLine":"4.5.4","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who's gone this morning?","sourceLine":"4.5.8","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who!\nOne ever near thee: call for Enobarbus,\nHe shall not hear thee; or from Caesar's camp\nSay 'I am none of thine.'","sourceLine":"4.5.9","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say'st thou?","sourceLine":"4.5.13","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir,\nHe is with Caesar.","sourceLine":"4.5.14","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, his chests and treasure\nHe has not with him.","sourceLine":"4.5.16","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is he gone?","sourceLine":"4.5.18","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most certain.","sourceLine":"4.5.19","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, Eros, send his treasure after; do it;\nDetain no jot, I charge thee: write to him--\nI will subscribe--gentle adieus and greetings;\nSay that I wish he never find more cause\nTo change a master. O, my fortunes have\nCorrupted honest men! Dispatch.--Enobarbus!","sourceLine":"4.5.20","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.5:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.5:13"}],"scene":5},{"id":"4.6","label":"Act 4 · SCENE VI. Alexandria. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's camp.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, AGRIPPA, with DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS, and others","id":"cleopatra:4.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go forth, Agrippa, and begin the fight:\nOur will is Antony be took alive;\nMake it so known.","sourceLine":"4.6.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:4.6:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, I shall.","sourceLine":"4.6.4","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:4.6:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:4.6:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The time of universal peace is near:\nProve this a prosperous day, the three-nook'd world\nShall bear the olive freely.","sourceLine":"4.6.5","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:4.6:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"cleopatra:4.6:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Antony\nIs come into the field.","sourceLine":"4.6.8","speaker":"Messenger","id":"cleopatra:4.6:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go charge Agrippa\nPlant those that have revolted in the van,\nThat Antony may seem to spend his fury\nUpon himself.","sourceLine":"4.6.10","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:4.6:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.6:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alexas did revolt; and went to Jewry on\nAffairs of Antony; there did persuade\nGreat Herod to incline himself to Caesar,\nAnd leave his master Antony: for this pains\nCaesar hath hang'd him. Canidius and the rest\nThat fell away have entertainment, but\nNo honourable trust. I have done ill;\nOf which I do accuse myself so sorely,\nThat I will joy no more.","sourceLine":"4.6.14","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.6:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Soldier of CAESAR's","id":"cleopatra:4.6:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Enobarbus, Antony\nHath after thee sent all thy treasure, with\nHis bounty overplus: the messenger\nCame on my guard; and at thy tent is now\nUnloading of his mules.","sourceLine":"4.6.23","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.6:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I give it you.","sourceLine":"4.6.28","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.6:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mock not, Enobarbus.\nI tell you true: best you safed the bringer\nOut of the host; I must attend mine office,\nOr would have done't myself. Your emperor\nContinues still a Jove.","sourceLine":"4.6.29","speaker":"Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.6:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:4.6:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am alone the villain of the earth,\nAnd feel I am so most. O Antony,\nThou mine of bounty, how wouldst thou have paid\nMy better service, when my turpitude\nThou dost so crown with gold! This blows my heart:\nIf swift thought break it not, a swifter mean\nShall outstrike thought: but thought will do't, I feel.\nI fight against thee! No: I will go seek\nSome ditch wherein to die; the foul'st best fits\nMy latter part of life.","sourceLine":"4.6.34","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.6:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:4.6:17"}],"scene":6},{"id":"4.7","label":"Act 4 · SCENE VII. Field of battle between the camps.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum. Drums and trumpets. Enter AGRIPPA and others","id":"cleopatra:4.7:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Retire, we have engaged ourselves too far:\nCaesar himself has work, and our oppression\nExceeds what we expected.","sourceLine":"4.7.1","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:4.7:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.7:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarums. Enter MARK ANTONY and SCARUS wounded","id":"cleopatra:4.7:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O my brave emperor, this is fought indeed!\nHad we done so at first, we had droven them home\nWith clouts about their heads.","sourceLine":"4.7.4","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.7:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou bleed'st apace.","sourceLine":"4.7.7","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.7:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I had a wound here that was like a T,\nBut now 'tis made an H.","sourceLine":"4.7.8","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.7:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They do retire.","sourceLine":"4.7.10","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.7:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll beat 'em into bench-holes: I have yet\nRoom for six scotches more.","sourceLine":"4.7.11","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.7:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.7:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are beaten, sir, and our advantage serves\nFor a fair victory.","sourceLine":"4.7.13","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.7:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us score their backs,\nAnd snatch 'em up, as we take hares, behind:\n'Tis sport to maul a runner.","sourceLine":"4.7.15","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.7:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will reward thee\nOnce for thy spritely comfort, and ten-fold\nFor thy good valour. Come thee on.","sourceLine":"4.7.18","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.7:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll halt after.","sourceLine":"4.7.21","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.7:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.7:15"}],"scene":7},{"id":"4.8","label":"Act 4 · SCENE VIII. Under the walls of Alexandria.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum. Enter MARK ANTONY, in a march; SCARUS, with others","id":"cleopatra:4.8:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We have beat him to his camp: run one before,\nAnd let the queen know of our gests. To-morrow,\nBefore the sun shall see 's, we'll spill the blood\nThat has to-day escaped. I thank you all;\nFor doughty-handed are you, and have fought\nNot as you served the cause, but as 't had been\nEach man's like mine; you have shown all Hectors.\nEnter the city, clip your wives, your friends,\nTell them your feats; whilst they with joyful tears\nWash the congealment from your wounds, and kiss\nThe honour'd gashes whole.","sourceLine":"4.8.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.8:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.8:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me thy hand","sourceLine":"4.8.12","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.8:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, attended","id":"cleopatra:4.8:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To this great fairy I'll commend thy acts,\nMake her thanks bless thee.","sourceLine":"4.8.13","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.8:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.8:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O thou day o' the world,\nChain mine arm'd neck; leap thou, attire and all,\nThrough proof of harness to my heart, and there\nRide on the pants triumphing!","sourceLine":"4.8.15","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.8:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord of lords!\nO infinite virtue, comest thou smiling from\nThe world's great snare uncaught?","sourceLine":"4.8.19","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.8:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My nightingale,\nWe have beat them to their beds. What, girl!\nthough grey\nDo something mingle with our younger brown, yet ha' we\nA brain that nourishes our nerves, and can\nGet goal for goal of youth. Behold this man;\nCommend unto his lips thy favouring hand:\nKiss it, my warrior: he hath fought to-day\nAs if a god, in hate of mankind, had\nDestroy'd in such a shape.","sourceLine":"4.8.22","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.8:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll give thee, friend,\nAn armour all of gold; it was a king's.","sourceLine":"4.8.32","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.8:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He has deserved it, were it carbuncled\nLike holy Phoebus' car. Give me thy hand:\nThrough Alexandria make a jolly march;\nBear our hack'd targets like the men that owe them:\nHad our great palace the capacity\nTo camp this host, we all would sup together,\nAnd drink carouses to the next day's fate,\nWhich promises royal peril. Trumpeters,\nWith brazen din blast you the city's ear;\nMake mingle with rattling tabourines;\nThat heaven and earth may strike their sounds together,\nApplauding our approach.","sourceLine":"4.8.34","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.8:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.8:13"}],"scene":8},{"id":"4.9","label":"Act 4 · SCENE IX. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's camp.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Sentinels at their post","id":"cleopatra:4.9:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If we be not relieved within this hour,\nWe must return to the court of guard: the night\nIs shiny; and they say we shall embattle\nBy the second hour i' the morn.","sourceLine":"4.9.1","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This last day was\nA shrewd one to's.","sourceLine":"4.9.5","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.9:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, bear me witness, night,--","sourceLine":"4.9.7","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.9:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What man is this?","sourceLine":"4.9.8","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stand close, and list him.","sourceLine":"4.9.9","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be witness to me, O thou blessed moon,\nWhen men revolted shall upon record\nBear hateful memory, poor Enobarbus did\nBefore thy face repent!","sourceLine":"4.9.10","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.9:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Enobarbus!","sourceLine":"4.9.14","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace!\nHark further.","sourceLine":"4.9.15","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O sovereign mistress of true melancholy,\nThe poisonous damp of night disponge upon me,\nThat life, a very rebel to my will,\nMay hang no longer on me: throw my heart\nAgainst the flint and hardness of my fault:\nWhich, being dried with grief, will break to powder,\nAnd finish all foul thoughts. O Antony,\nNobler than my revolt is infamous,\nForgive me in thine own particular;\nBut let the world rank me in register\nA master-leaver and a fugitive:\nO Antony! O Antony!","sourceLine":"4.9.17","speaker":"DOMITIUS ENOBARBUS","id":"cleopatra:4.9:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Dies","id":"cleopatra:4.9:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let's speak To him.","sourceLine":"4.9.29","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let's hear him, for the things he speaks\nMay concern Caesar.","sourceLine":"4.9.30","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let's do so. But he sleeps.","sourceLine":"4.9.32","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Swoons rather; for so bad a prayer as his\nWas never yet for sleep.","sourceLine":"4.9.33","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go we to him.","sourceLine":"4.9.35","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Awake, sir, awake; speak to us.","sourceLine":"4.9.36","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear you, sir?","sourceLine":"4.9.37","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The hand of death hath raught him.","sourceLine":"4.9.38","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Drums afar off","id":"cleopatra:4.9:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark! the drums\nDemurely wake the sleepers. Let us bear him\nTo the court of guard; he is of note: our hour\nIs fully out.","sourceLine":"4.9.39","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come on, then;\nHe may recover yet.","sourceLine":"4.9.43","speaker":"Third Soldier","id":"cleopatra:4.9:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt with the body","id":"cleopatra:4.9:24"}],"scene":9},{"id":"4.10","label":"Act 4 · SCENE X. Between the two camps.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and SCARUS, with their Army","id":"cleopatra:4.10:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Their preparation is to-day by sea;\nWe please them not by land.","sourceLine":"4.10.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.10:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For both, my lord.","sourceLine":"4.10.3","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.10:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would they'ld fight i' the fire or i' the air;\nWe'ld fight there too. But this it is; our foot\nUpon the hills adjoining to the city\nShall stay with us: order for sea is given;\nThey have put forth the haven\nWhere their appointment we may best discover,\nAnd look on their endeavour.","sourceLine":"4.10.4","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.10:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.10:5"}],"scene":10},{"id":"4.11","label":"Act 4 · SCENE XI. Another part of the same.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, and his Army","id":"cleopatra:4.11:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But being charged, we will be still by land,\nWhich, as I take't, we shall; for his best force\nIs forth to man his galleys. To the vales,\nAnd hold our best advantage.","sourceLine":"4.11.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:4.11:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.11:3"}],"scene":11},{"id":"4.12","label":"Act 4 · SCENE XII. Another part of the same.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.12:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet they are not join'd: where yond pine\ndoes stand,\nI shall discover all: I'll bring thee word\nStraight, how 'tis like to go.","sourceLine":"4.12.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.12:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:4.12:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Swallows have built\nIn Cleopatra's sails their nests: the augurers\nSay they know not, they cannot tell; look grimly,\nAnd dare not speak their knowledge. Antony\nIs valiant, and dejected; and, by starts,\nHis fretted fortunes give him hope, and fear,\nOf what he has, and has not.","sourceLine":"4.12.5","speaker":"SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.12:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum afar off, as at a sea-fight","id":"cleopatra:4.12:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.12:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All is lost;\nThis foul Egyptian hath betrayed me:\nMy fleet hath yielded to the foe; and yonder\nThey cast their caps up and carouse together\nLike friends long lost. Triple-turn'd whore!\n'tis thou\nHast sold me to this novice; and my heart\nMakes only wars on thee. Bid them all fly;\nFor when I am revenged upon my charm,\nI have done all. Bid them all fly; begone.","sourceLine":"4.12.12","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.12:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit SCARUS","id":"cleopatra:4.12:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O sun, thy uprise shall I see no more:\nFortune and Antony part here; even here\nDo we shake hands. All come to this? The hearts\nThat spaniel'd me at heels, to whom I gave\nTheir wishes, do discandy, melt their sweets\nOn blossoming Caesar; and this pine is bark'd,\nThat overtopp'd them all. Betray'd I am:\nO this false soul of Egypt! this grave charm,--\nWhose eye beck'd forth my wars, and call'd them home;\nWhose bosom was my crownet, my chief end,--\nLike a right gipsy, hath, at fast and loose,\nBeguiled me to the very heart of loss.\nWhat, Eros, Eros!","sourceLine":"4.12.22","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.12:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.12:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, thou spell! Avaunt!","sourceLine":"4.12.35","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.12:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why is my lord enraged against his love?","sourceLine":"4.12.36","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.12:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Vanish, or I shall give thee thy deserving,\nAnd blemish Caesar's triumph. Let him take thee,\nAnd hoist thee up to the shouting plebeians:\nFollow his chariot, like the greatest spot\nOf all thy sex; most monster-like, be shown\nFor poor'st diminutives, for doits; and let\nPatient Octavia plough thy visage up\nWith her prepared nails.","sourceLine":"4.12.37","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.12:13"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.12:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis well thou'rt gone,\nIf it be well to live; but better 'twere\nThou fell'st into my fury, for one death\nMight have prevented many. Eros, ho!\nThe shirt of Nessus is upon me: teach me,\nAlcides, thou mine ancestor, thy rage:\nLet me lodge Lichas on the horns o' the moon;\nAnd with those hands, that grasp'd the heaviest club,\nSubdue my worthiest self. The witch shall die:\nTo the young Roman boy she hath sold me, and I fall\nUnder this plot; she dies for't. Eros, ho!","sourceLine":"4.12.45","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.12:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:4.12:16"}],"scene":12},{"id":"4.13","label":"Act 4 · SCENE XIII. Alexandria. Cleopatra's palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, CHARMIAN, IRAS, and MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.13:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Help me, my women! O, he is more mad\nThan Telamon for his shield; the boar of Thessaly\nWas never so emboss'd.","sourceLine":"4.13.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.13:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To the monument!\nThere lock yourself, and send him word you are dead.\nThe soul and body rive not more in parting\nThan greatness going off.","sourceLine":"4.13.4","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.13:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To the monument!\nMardian, go tell him I have slain myself;\nSay, that the last I spoke was 'Antony,'\nAnd word it, prithee, piteously: hence, Mardian,\nAnd bring me how he takes my death.\nTo the monument!","sourceLine":"4.13.8","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.13:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:4.13:5"}],"scene":13},{"id":"4.14","label":"Act 4 · SCENE XIV. The same. Another room.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARK ANTONY and EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Eros, thou yet behold'st me?","sourceLine":"4.14.1","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, noble lord.","sourceLine":"4.14.2","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sometimes we see a cloud that's dragonish;\nA vapour sometime like a bear or lion,\nA tower'd citadel, a pendent rock,\nA forked mountain, or blue promontory\nWith trees upon't, that nod unto the world,\nAnd mock our eyes with air: thou hast seen\nthese signs;\nThey are black vesper's pageants.","sourceLine":"4.14.3","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my lord,","sourceLine":"4.14.11","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That which is now a horse, even with a thought\nThe rack dislimns, and makes it indistinct,\nAs water is in water.","sourceLine":"4.14.12","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It does, my lord.","sourceLine":"4.14.15","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My good knave Eros, now thy captain is\nEven such a body: here I am Antony:\nYet cannot hold this visible shape, my knave.\nI made these wars for Egypt: and the queen,--\nWhose heart I thought I had, for she had mine;\nWhich whilst it was mine had annex'd unto't\nA million more, now lost,--she, Eros, has\nPack'd cards with Caesar, and false-play'd my glory\nUnto an enemy's triumph.\nNay, weep not, gentle Eros; there is left us\nOurselves to end ourselves.","sourceLine":"4.14.16","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.14:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O! thy vile lady!\nShe has robb'd me of my sword.","sourceLine":"4.14.27","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, Antony;\nMy mistress loved thee, and her fortunes mingled\nWith thine entirely.","sourceLine":"4.14.29","speaker":"MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.14:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hence, saucy eunuch; peace!\nShe hath betray'd me and shall die the death.","sourceLine":"4.14.32","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Death of one person can be paid but once,\nAnd that she has discharged: what thou wouldst do\nIs done unto thy hand: the last she spake\nWas 'Antony! most noble Antony!'\nThen in the midst a tearing groan did break\nThe name of Antony; it was divided\nBetween her heart and lips: she render'd life,\nThy name so buried in her.","sourceLine":"4.14.34","speaker":"MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.14:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dead, then?","sourceLine":"4.14.42","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dead.","sourceLine":"4.14.43","speaker":"MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.14:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Unarm, Eros; the long day's task is done,\nAnd we must sleep.","sourceLine":"4.14.44","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:16"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.14:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That thou depart'st hence safe,\nDoes pay thy labour richly; go.","sourceLine":"4.14.46","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit MARDIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.14:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Off, pluck off:\nThe seven-fold shield of Ajax cannot keep\nThe battery from my heart. O, cleave, my sides!\nHeart, once be stronger than thy continent,\nCrack thy frail case! Apace, Eros, apace.\nNo more a soldier: bruised pieces, go;\nYou have been nobly borne. From me awhile.","sourceLine":"4.14.48","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will o'ertake thee, Cleopatra, and\nWeep for my pardon. So it must be, for now\nAll length is torture: since the torch is out,\nLie down, and stray no farther: now all labour\nMars what it does; yea, very force entangles\nItself with strength: seal then, and all is done.\nEros!--I come, my queen:--Eros!--Stay for me:\nWhere souls do couch on flowers, we'll hand in hand,\nAnd with our sprightly port make the ghosts gaze:\nDido and her AEneas shall want troops,\nAnd all the haunt be ours. Come, Eros, Eros!","sourceLine":"4.14.55","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What would my lord?","sourceLine":"4.14.66","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Since Cleopatra died,\nI have lived in such dishonour, that the gods\nDetest my baseness. I, that with my sword\nQuarter'd the world, and o'er green Neptune's back\nWith ships made cities, condemn myself to lack\nThe courage of a woman; less noble mind\nThan she which by her death our Caesar tells\n'I am conqueror of myself.' Thou art sworn, Eros,\nThat, when the exigent should come, which now\nIs come indeed, when I should see behind me\nThe inevitable prosecution of\nDisgrace and horror, that, on my command,\nThou then wouldst kill me: do't; the time is come:\nThou strikest not me, 'tis Caesar thou defeat'st.\nPut colour in thy cheek.","sourceLine":"4.14.67","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods withhold me!\nShall I do that which all the Parthian darts,\nThough enemy, lost aim, and could not?","sourceLine":"4.14.82","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Eros,\nWouldst thou be window'd in great Rome and see\nThy master thus with pleach'd arms, bending down\nHis corrigible neck, his face subdued\nTo penetrative shame, whilst the wheel'd seat\nOf fortunate Caesar, drawn before him, branded\nHis baseness that ensued?","sourceLine":"4.14.85","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would not see't.","sourceLine":"4.14.92","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, then; for with a wound I must be cured.\nDraw that thy honest sword, which thou hast worn\nMost useful for thy country.","sourceLine":"4.14.93","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, sir, pardon me!","sourceLine":"4.14.96","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When I did make thee free, sworest thou not then\nTo do this when I bade thee? Do it at once;\nOr thy precedent services are all\nBut accidents unpurposed. Draw, and come.","sourceLine":"4.14.97","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Turn from me, then, that noble countenance,\nWherein the worship of the whole world lies.","sourceLine":"4.14.101","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lo thee!","sourceLine":"4.14.103","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Turning from him","id":"cleopatra:4.14:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My sword is drawn.","sourceLine":"4.14.104","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then let it do at once\nThe thing why thou hast drawn it.","sourceLine":"4.14.105","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My dear master,\nMy captain, and my emperor, let me say,\nBefore I strike this bloody stroke, farewell.","sourceLine":"4.14.107","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis said, man; and farewell.","sourceLine":"4.14.110","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, great chief. Shall I strike now?","sourceLine":"4.14.111","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, Eros.","sourceLine":"4.14.112","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, there then: thus I do escape the sorrow\nOf Antony's death.","sourceLine":"4.14.113","speaker":"EROS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Kills himself","id":"cleopatra:4.14:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thrice-nobler than myself!\nThou teachest me, O valiant Eros, what\nI should, and thou couldst not. My queen and Eros\nHave by their brave instruction got upon me\nA nobleness in record: but I will be\nA bridegroom in my death, and run into't\nAs to a lover's bed. Come, then; and, Eros,\nThy master dies thy scholar: to do thus","sourceLine":"4.14.115","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Falling on his sword","id":"cleopatra:4.14:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I learn'd of thee. How! not dead? not dead?\nThe guard, ho! O, dispatch me!","sourceLine":"4.14.123","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:45"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DERCETAS and Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's the noise?","sourceLine":"4.14.125","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have done my work in, friends: O, make an end\nOf what I have begun.","sourceLine":"4.14.126","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The star is fall'n.","sourceLine":"4.14.128","speaker":"Second Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And time is at his period.","sourceLine":"4.14.129","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, and woe!","sourceLine":"4.14.130","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:4.14:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him that loves me strike me dead.","sourceLine":"4.14.131","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not I.","sourceLine":"4.14.132","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor I.","sourceLine":"4.14.133","speaker":"Second Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor any one.","sourceLine":"4.14.134","speaker":"Third Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:55"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy death and fortunes bid thy followers fly.\nThis sword but shown to Caesar, with this tidings,\nShall enter me with him.","sourceLine":"4.14.135","speaker":"DERCETAS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:57"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.14:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where's Antony?","sourceLine":"4.14.138","speaker":"DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.14:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There, Diomed there.","sourceLine":"4.14.139","speaker":"DERCETAS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lives he?\nWilt thou not answer, man?","sourceLine":"4.14.140","speaker":"DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.14:61"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit DERCETAS","id":"cleopatra:4.14:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Art thou there, Diomed? Draw thy sword, and give me\nSufficing strokes for death.","sourceLine":"4.14.142","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most absolute lord,\nMy mistress Cleopatra sent me to thee.","sourceLine":"4.14.144","speaker":"DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.14:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When did she send thee?","sourceLine":"4.14.146","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, my lord.","sourceLine":"4.14.147","speaker":"DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.14:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is she?","sourceLine":"4.14.148","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lock'd in her monument. She had a prophesying fear\nOf what hath come to pass: for when she saw--\nWhich never shall be found--you did suspect\nShe had disposed with Caesar, and that your rage\nWould not be purged, she sent you word she was dead;\nBut, fearing since how it might work, hath sent\nMe to proclaim the truth; and I am come,\nI dread, too late.","sourceLine":"4.14.149","speaker":"DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.14:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Too late, good Diomed: call my guard, I prithee.","sourceLine":"4.14.157","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, ho, the emperor's guard! The guard, what, ho!\nCome, your lord calls!","sourceLine":"4.14.158","speaker":"DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.14:70"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter four or five of the Guard of MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bear me, good friends, where Cleopatra bides;\n'Tis the last service that I shall command you.","sourceLine":"4.14.160","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Woe, woe are we, sir, you may not live to wear\nAll your true followers out.","sourceLine":"4.14.162","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.14:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most heavy day!","sourceLine":"4.14.164","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:4.14:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, good my fellows, do not please sharp fate\nTo grace it with your sorrows: bid that welcome\nWhich comes to punish us, and we punish it\nSeeming to bear it lightly. Take me up:\nI have led you oft: carry me now, good friends,\nAnd have my thanks for all.","sourceLine":"4.14.165","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:75"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt, bearing MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.14:76"}],"scene":14},{"id":"4.15","label":"Act 4 · SCENE XV. The same. A monument.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA and her maids aloft, with CHARMIAN and IRAS","id":"cleopatra:4.15:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Charmian, I will never go from hence.","sourceLine":"4.15.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be comforted, dear madam.","sourceLine":"4.15.2","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.15:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, I will not:\nAll strange and terrible events are welcome,\nBut comforts we despise; our size of sorrow,\nProportion'd to our cause, must be as great\nAs that which makes it.","sourceLine":"4.15.3","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter, below, DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.15:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! is he dead?","sourceLine":"4.15.8","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His death's upon him, but not dead.\nLook out o' the other side your monument;\nHis guard have brought him thither.","sourceLine":"4.15.9","speaker":"DIOMEDES","id":"cleopatra:4.15:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter, below, MARK ANTONY, borne by the Guard","id":"cleopatra:4.15:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O sun,\nBurn the great sphere thou movest in!\ndarkling stand\nThe varying shore o' the world. O Antony,\nAntony, Antony! Help, Charmian, help, Iras, help;\nHelp, friends below; let's draw him hither.","sourceLine":"4.15.12","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace!\nNot Caesar's valour hath o'erthrown Antony,\nBut Antony's hath triumph'd on itself.","sourceLine":"4.15.18","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.15:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So it should be, that none but Antony\nShould conquer Antony; but woe 'tis so!","sourceLine":"4.15.21","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am dying, Egypt, dying; only\nI here importune death awhile, until\nOf many thousand kisses the poor last\nI lay up thy lips.","sourceLine":"4.15.23","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.15:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I dare not, dear,--\nDear my lord, pardon,--I dare not,\nLest I be taken: not the imperious show\nOf the full-fortuned Caesar ever shall\nBe brooch'd with me; if knife, drugs,\nserpents, have\nEdge, sting, or operation, I am safe:\nYour wife Octavia, with her modest eyes\nAnd still conclusion, shall acquire no honour\nDemuring upon me. But come, come, Antony,--\nHelp me, my women,--we must draw thee up:\nAssist, good friends.","sourceLine":"4.15.27","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, quick, or I am gone.","sourceLine":"4.15.39","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.15:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here's sport indeed! How heavy weighs my lord!\nOur strength is all gone into heaviness,\nThat makes the weight: had I great Juno's power,\nThe strong-wing'd Mercury should fetch thee up,\nAnd set thee by Jove's side. Yet come a little,--\nWishes were ever fools,--O, come, come, come;","sourceLine":"4.15.40","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:15"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They heave MARK ANTONY aloft to CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And welcome, welcome! die where thou hast lived:\nQuicken with kissing: had my lips that power,\nThus would I wear them out.","sourceLine":"4.15.46","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A heavy sight!","sourceLine":"4.15.49","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:4.15:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am dying, Egypt, dying:\nGive me some wine, and let me speak a little.","sourceLine":"4.15.50","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.15:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, let me speak; and let me rail so high,\nThat the false housewife Fortune break her wheel,\nProvoked by my offence.","sourceLine":"4.15.52","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"One word, sweet queen:\nOf Caesar seek your honour, with your safety. O!","sourceLine":"4.15.55","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.15:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They do not go together.","sourceLine":"4.15.57","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gentle, hear me:\nNone about Caesar trust but Proculeius.","sourceLine":"4.15.58","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.15:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My resolution and my hands I'll trust;\nNone about Caesar.","sourceLine":"4.15.60","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The miserable change now at my end\nLament nor sorrow at; but please your thoughts\nIn feeding them with those my former fortunes\nWherein I lived, the greatest prince o' the world,\nThe noblest; and do now not basely die,\nNot cowardly put off my helmet to\nMy countryman,--a Roman by a Roman\nValiantly vanquish'd. Now my spirit is going;\nI can no more.","sourceLine":"4.15.62","speaker":"MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:4.15:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Noblest of men, woo't die?\nHast thou no care of me? shall I abide\nIn this dull world, which in thy absence is\nNo better than a sty? O, see, my women,","sourceLine":"4.15.71","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"MARK ANTONY dies","id":"cleopatra:4.15:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The crown o' the earth doth melt. My lord!\nO, wither'd is the garland of the war,\nThe soldier's pole is fall'n: young boys and girls\nAre level now with men; the odds is gone,\nAnd there is nothing left remarkable\nBeneath the visiting moon.","sourceLine":"4.15.75","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Faints","id":"cleopatra:4.15:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, quietness, lady!","sourceLine":"4.15.81","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.15:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is dead too, our sovereign.","sourceLine":"4.15.82","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:4.15:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lady!","sourceLine":"4.15.83","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.15:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam!","sourceLine":"4.15.84","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:4.15:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O madam, madam, madam!","sourceLine":"4.15.85","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.15:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Royal Egypt, Empress!","sourceLine":"4.15.86","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:4.15:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace, peace, Iras!","sourceLine":"4.15.87","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:4.15:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more, but e'en a woman, and commanded\nBy such poor passion as the maid that milks\nAnd does the meanest chares. It were for me\nTo throw my sceptre at the injurious gods;\nTo tell them that this world did equal theirs\nTill they had stol'n our jewel. All's but naught;\nPatience is scottish, and impatience does\nBecome a dog that's mad: then is it sin\nTo rush into the secret house of death,\nEre death dare come to us? How do you, women?\nWhat, what! good cheer! Why, how now, Charmian!\nMy noble girls! Ah, women, women, look,\nOur lamp is spent, it's out! Good sirs, take heart:\nWe'll bury him; and then, what's brave,\nwhat's noble,\nLet's do it after the high Roman fashion,\nAnd make death proud to take us. Come, away:\nThis case of that huge spirit now is cold:\nAh, women, women! come; we have no friend\nBut resolution, and the briefest end.","sourceLine":"4.15.88","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:4.15:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt; those above bearing off MARK ANTONY's body","id":"cleopatra:4.15:38"}],"scene":15},{"id":"5.1","label":"Act 5 · SCENE I. Alexandria. OCTAVIUS CAESAR's camp.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, AGRIPPA, DOLABELLA, MECAENAS, GALLUS, PROCULEIUS, and others, his council of war","id":"cleopatra:5.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go to him, Dolabella, bid him yield;\nBeing so frustrate, tell him he mocks\nThe pauses that he makes.","sourceLine":"5.1.1","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, I shall.","sourceLine":"5.1.4","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.1:3"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:5.1:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DERCETAS, with the sword of MARK ANTONY","id":"cleopatra:5.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wherefore is that? and what art thou that darest\nAppear thus to us?","sourceLine":"5.1.5","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am call'd Dercetas;\nMark Antony I served, who best was worthy\nBest to be served: whilst he stood up and spoke,\nHe was my master; and I wore my life\nTo spend upon his haters. If thou please\nTo take me to thee, as I was to him\nI'll be to Caesar; if thou pleasest not,\nI yield thee up my life.","sourceLine":"5.1.7","speaker":"DERCETAS","id":"cleopatra:5.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is't thou say'st?","sourceLine":"5.1.15","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I say, O Caesar, Antony is dead.","sourceLine":"5.1.16","speaker":"DERCETAS","id":"cleopatra:5.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The breaking of so great a thing should make\nA greater crack: the round world\nShould have shook lions into civil streets,\nAnd citizens to their dens: the death of Antony\nIs not a single doom; in the name lay\nA moiety of the world.","sourceLine":"5.1.17","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He is dead, Caesar:\nNot by a public minister of justice,\nNor by a hired knife; but that self hand,\nWhich writ his honour in the acts it did,\nHath, with the courage which the heart did lend it,\nSplitted the heart. This is his sword;\nI robb'd his wound of it; behold it stain'd\nWith his most noble blood.","sourceLine":"5.1.23","speaker":"DERCETAS","id":"cleopatra:5.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look you sad, friends?\nThe gods rebuke me, but it is tidings\nTo wash the eyes of kings.","sourceLine":"5.1.31","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And strange it is,\nThat nature must compel us to lament\nOur most persisted deeds.","sourceLine":"5.1.34","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:5.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His taints and honours\nWaged equal with him.","sourceLine":"5.1.37","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:5.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A rarer spirit never\nDid steer humanity: but you, gods, will give us\nSome faults to make us men. Caesar is touch'd.","sourceLine":"5.1.39","speaker":"AGRIPPA","id":"cleopatra:5.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When such a spacious mirror's set before him,\nHe needs must see himself.","sourceLine":"5.1.42","speaker":"MECAENAS","id":"cleopatra:5.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Antony!\nI have follow'd thee to this; but we do lance\nDiseases in our bodies: I must perforce\nHave shown to thee such a declining day,\nOr look on thine; we could not stall together\nIn the whole world: but yet let me lament,\nWith tears as sovereign as the blood of hearts,\nThat thou, my brother, my competitor\nIn top of all design, my mate in empire,\nFriend and companion in the front of war,\nThe arm of mine own body, and the heart\nWhere mine his thoughts did kindle,--that our stars,\nUnreconciliable, should divide\nOur equalness to this. Hear me, good friends--\nBut I will tell you at some meeter season:","sourceLine":"5.1.44","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter an Egyptian","id":"cleopatra:5.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The business of this man looks out of him;\nWe'll hear him what he says. Whence are you?","sourceLine":"5.1.59","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A poor Egyptian yet. The queen my mistress,\nConfined in all she has, her monument,\nOf thy intents desires instruction,\nThat she preparedly may frame herself\nTo the way she's forced to.","sourceLine":"5.1.61","speaker":"Egyptian","id":"cleopatra:5.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bid her have good heart:\nShe soon shall know of us, by some of ours,\nHow honourable and how kindly we\nDetermine for her; for Caesar cannot live\nTo be ungentle.","sourceLine":"5.1.66","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So the gods preserve thee!","sourceLine":"5.1.71","speaker":"Egyptian","id":"cleopatra:5.1:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:5.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come hither, Proculeius. Go and say,\nWe purpose her no shame: give her what comforts\nThe quality of her passion shall require,\nLest, in her greatness, by some mortal stroke\nShe do defeat us; for her life in Rome\nWould be eternal in our triumph: go,\nAnd with your speediest bring us what she says,\nAnd how you find of her.","sourceLine":"5.1.72","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, I shall.","sourceLine":"5.1.80","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.1:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:5.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gallus, go you along.","sourceLine":"5.1.81","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:27"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit GALLUS","id":"cleopatra:5.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where's Dolabella,\nTo second Proculeius?","sourceLine":"5.1.82","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dolabella!","sourceLine":"5.1.84","speaker":"All","id":"cleopatra:5.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him alone, for I remember now\nHow he's employ'd: he shall in time be ready.\nGo with me to my tent; where you shall see\nHow hardly I was drawn into this war;\nHow calm and gentle I proceeded still\nIn all my writings: go with me, and see\nWhat I can show in this.","sourceLine":"5.1.85","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.1:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:5.1:32"}],"scene":1},{"id":"5.2","label":"Act 5 · SCENE II. Alexandria. A room in the monument.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/cleopatra/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter CLEOPATRA, CHARMIAN, and IRAS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My desolation does begin to make\nA better life. 'Tis paltry to be Caesar;\nNot being Fortune, he's but Fortune's knave,\nA minister of her will: and it is great\nTo do that thing that ends all other deeds;\nWhich shackles accidents and bolts up change;\nWhich sleeps, and never palates more the dug,\nThe beggar's nurse and Caesar's.","sourceLine":"5.2.1","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter, to the gates of the monument, PROCULEIUS, GALLUS and Soldiers","id":"cleopatra:5.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar sends greeting to the Queen of Egypt;\nAnd bids thee study on what fair demands\nThou mean'st to have him grant thee.","sourceLine":"5.2.9","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's thy name?","sourceLine":"5.2.12","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My name is Proculeius.","sourceLine":"5.2.13","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Antony\nDid tell me of you, bade me trust you; but\nI do not greatly care to be deceived,\nThat have no use for trusting. If your master\nWould have a queen his beggar, you must tell him,\nThat majesty, to keep decorum, must\nNo less beg than a kingdom: if he please\nTo give me conquer'd Egypt for my son,\nHe gives me so much of mine own, as I\nWill kneel to him with thanks.","sourceLine":"5.2.14","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be of good cheer;\nYou're fall'n into a princely hand, fear nothing:\nMake your full reference freely to my lord,\nWho is so full of grace, that it flows over\nOn all that need: let me report to him\nYour sweet dependency; and you shall find\nA conqueror that will pray in aid for kindness,\nWhere he for grace is kneel'd to.","sourceLine":"5.2.24","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray you, tell him\nI am his fortune's vassal, and I send him\nThe greatness he has got. I hourly learn\nA doctrine of obedience; and would gladly\nLook him i' the face.","sourceLine":"5.2.32","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This I'll report, dear lady.\nHave comfort, for I know your plight is pitied\nOf him that caused it.","sourceLine":"5.2.37","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You see how easily she may be surprised:","sourceLine":"5.2.40","speaker":"GALLUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Here PROCULEIUS and two of the Guard ascend the monument by a ladder placed against a window, and, having descended, come behind CLEOPATRA. Some of the Guard unbar and open the gates","id":"cleopatra:5.2:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To PROCULEIUS and the Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Guard her till Caesar come.","sourceLine":"5.2.41","speaker":"GALLUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:14"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:5.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Royal queen!","sourceLine":"5.2.42","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Cleopatra! thou art taken, queen:","sourceLine":"5.2.43","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Quick, quick, good hands.","sourceLine":"5.2.44","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Drawing a dagger","id":"cleopatra:5.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold, worthy lady, hold:","sourceLine":"5.2.45","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Seizes and disarms her","id":"cleopatra:5.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do not yourself such wrong, who are in this\nRelieved, but not betray'd.","sourceLine":"5.2.46","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, of death too,\nThat rids our dogs of languish?","sourceLine":"5.2.48","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cleopatra,\nDo not abuse my master's bounty by\nThe undoing of yourself: let the world see\nHis nobleness well acted, which your death\nWill never let come forth.","sourceLine":"5.2.50","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where art thou, death?\nCome hither, come! come, come, and take a queen\nWorthy many babes and beggars!","sourceLine":"5.2.55","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, temperance, lady!","sourceLine":"5.2.58","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, I will eat no meat, I'll not drink, sir;\nIf idle talk will once be necessary,\nI'll not sleep neither: this mortal house I'll ruin,\nDo Caesar what he can. Know, sir, that I\nWill not wait pinion'd at your master's court;\nNor once be chastised with the sober eye\nOf dull Octavia. Shall they hoist me up\nAnd show me to the shouting varletry\nOf censuring Rome? Rather a ditch in Egypt\nBe gentle grave unto me! rather on Nilus' mud\nLay me stark naked, and let the water-flies\nBlow me into abhorring! rather make\nMy country's high pyramides my gibbet,\nAnd hang me up in chains!","sourceLine":"5.2.59","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You do extend\nThese thoughts of horror further than you shall\nFind cause in Caesar.","sourceLine":"5.2.73","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Proculeius,\nWhat thou hast done thy master Caesar knows,\nAnd he hath sent for thee: for the queen,\nI'll take her to my guard.","sourceLine":"5.2.76","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, Dolabella,\nIt shall content me best: be gentle to her.","sourceLine":"5.2.80","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To Caesar I will speak what you shall please,\nIf you'll employ me to him.","sourceLine":"5.2.82","speaker":"PROCULEIUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Say, I would die.","sourceLine":"5.2.84","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:34"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt PROCULEIUS and Soldiers","id":"cleopatra:5.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most noble empress, you have heard of me?","sourceLine":"5.2.85","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot tell.","sourceLine":"5.2.86","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Assuredly you know me.","sourceLine":"5.2.87","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No matter, sir, what I have heard or known.\nYou laugh when boys or women tell their dreams;\nIs't not your trick?","sourceLine":"5.2.88","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I understand not, madam.","sourceLine":"5.2.91","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I dream'd there was an Emperor Antony:\nO, such another sleep, that I might see\nBut such another man!","sourceLine":"5.2.92","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If it might please ye,--","sourceLine":"5.2.95","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His face was as the heavens; and therein stuck\nA sun and moon, which kept their course,\nand lighted\nThe little O, the earth.","sourceLine":"5.2.96","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most sovereign creature,--","sourceLine":"5.2.100","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His legs bestrid the ocean: his rear'd arm\nCrested the world: his voice was propertied\nAs all the tuned spheres, and that to friends;\nBut when he meant to quail and shake the orb,\nHe was as rattling thunder. For his bounty,\nThere was no winter in't; an autumn 'twas\nThat grew the more by reaping: his delights\nWere dolphin-like; they show'd his back above\nThe element they lived in: in his livery\nWalk'd crowns and crownets; realms and islands were\nAs plates dropp'd from his pocket.","sourceLine":"5.2.101","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cleopatra!","sourceLine":"5.2.112","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Think you there was, or might be, such a man\nAs this I dream'd of?","sourceLine":"5.2.113","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gentle madam, no.","sourceLine":"5.2.115","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You lie, up to the hearing of the gods.\nBut, if there be, or ever were, one such,\nIt's past the size of dreaming: nature wants stuff\nTo vie strange forms with fancy; yet, to imagine\nAnd Antony, were nature's piece 'gainst fancy,\nCondemning shadows quite.","sourceLine":"5.2.116","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hear me, good madam.\nYour loss is as yourself, great; and you bear it\nAs answering to the weight: would I might never\nO'ertake pursued success, but I do feel,\nBy the rebound of yours, a grief that smites\nMy very heart at root.","sourceLine":"5.2.122","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank you, sir,\nKnow you what Caesar means to do with me?","sourceLine":"5.2.128","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am loath to tell you what I would you knew.","sourceLine":"5.2.130","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, pray you, sir,--","sourceLine":"5.2.131","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Though he be honourable,--","sourceLine":"5.2.132","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He'll lead me, then, in triumph?","sourceLine":"5.2.133","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, he will; I know't.","sourceLine":"5.2.134","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:56"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish, and shout within, 'Make way there: Octavius Caesar!'","id":"cleopatra:5.2:57"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR, GALLUS, PROCULEIUS, MECAENAS, SELEUCUS, and others of his Train","id":"cleopatra:5.2:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which is the Queen of Egypt?","sourceLine":"5.2.135","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is the emperor, madam.","sourceLine":"5.2.136","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:60"},{"kind":"stage","text":"CLEOPATRA kneels","id":"cleopatra:5.2:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Arise, you shall not kneel:\nI pray you, rise; rise, Egypt.","sourceLine":"5.2.137","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, the gods\nWill have it thus; my master and my lord\nI must obey.","sourceLine":"5.2.139","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take to you no hard thoughts:\nThe record of what injuries you did us,\nThough written in our flesh, we shall remember\nAs things but done by chance.","sourceLine":"5.2.142","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sole sir o' the world,\nI cannot project mine own cause so well\nTo make it clear; but do confess I have\nBeen laden with like frailties which before\nHave often shamed our sex.","sourceLine":"5.2.146","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cleopatra, know,\nWe will extenuate rather than enforce:\nIf you apply yourself to our intents,\nWhich towards you are most gentle, you shall find\nA benefit in this change; but if you seek\nTo lay on me a cruelty, by taking\nAntony's course, you shall bereave yourself\nOf my good purposes, and put your children\nTo that destruction which I'll guard them from,\nIf thereon you rely. I'll take my leave.","sourceLine":"5.2.151","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And may, through all the world: 'tis yours; and we,\nYour scutcheons and your signs of conquest, shall\nHang in what place you please. Here, my good lord.","sourceLine":"5.2.161","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall advise me in all for Cleopatra.","sourceLine":"5.2.164","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is the brief of money, plate, and jewels,\nI am possess'd of: 'tis exactly valued;\nNot petty things admitted. Where's Seleucus?","sourceLine":"5.2.165","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, madam.","sourceLine":"5.2.168","speaker":"SELEUCUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is my treasurer: let him speak, my lord,\nUpon his peril, that I have reserved\nTo myself nothing. Speak the truth, Seleucus.","sourceLine":"5.2.169","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam,\nI had rather seal my lips, than, to my peril,\nSpeak that which is not.","sourceLine":"5.2.172","speaker":"SELEUCUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What have I kept back?","sourceLine":"5.2.175","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Enough to purchase what you have made known.","sourceLine":"5.2.176","speaker":"SELEUCUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, blush not, Cleopatra; I approve\nYour wisdom in the deed.","sourceLine":"5.2.177","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"See, Caesar! O, behold,\nHow pomp is follow'd! mine will now be yours;\nAnd, should we shift estates, yours would be mine.\nThe ingratitude of this Seleucus does\nEven make me wild: O slave, of no more trust\nThan love that's hired! What, goest thou back? thou shalt\nGo back, I warrant thee; but I'll catch thine eyes,\nThough they had wings: slave, soulless villain, dog!\nO rarely base!","sourceLine":"5.2.179","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good queen, let us entreat you.","sourceLine":"5.2.188","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Caesar, what a wounding shame is this,\nThat thou, vouchsafing here to visit me,\nDoing the honour of thy lordliness\nTo one so meek, that mine own servant should\nParcel the sum of my disgraces by\nAddition of his envy! Say, good Caesar,\nThat I some lady trifles have reserved,\nImmoment toys, things of such dignity\nAs we greet modern friends withal; and say,\nSome nobler token I have kept apart\nFor Livia and Octavia, to induce\nTheir mediation; must I be unfolded\nWith one that I have bred? The gods! it smites me\nBeneath the fall I have.","sourceLine":"5.2.189","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:78"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To SELEUCUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Prithee, go hence;\nOr I shall show the cinders of my spirits\nThrough the ashes of my chance: wert thou a man,\nThou wouldst have mercy on me.","sourceLine":"5.2.203","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Forbear, Seleucus.","sourceLine":"5.2.207","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:81"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit SELEUCUS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be it known, that we, the greatest, are misthought\nFor things that others do; and, when we fall,\nWe answer others' merits in our name,\nAre therefore to be pitied.","sourceLine":"5.2.208","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cleopatra,\nNot what you have reserved, nor what acknowledged,\nPut we i' the roll of conquest: still be't yours,\nBestow it at your pleasure; and believe,\nCaesar's no merchant, to make prize with you\nOf things that merchants sold. Therefore be cheer'd;\nMake not your thoughts your prisons: no, dear queen;\nFor we intend so to dispose you as\nYourself shall give us counsel. Feed, and sleep:\nOur care and pity is so much upon you,\nThat we remain your friend; and so, adieu.","sourceLine":"5.2.212","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My master, and my lord!","sourceLine":"5.2.223","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not so. Adieu.","sourceLine":"5.2.224","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:86"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt OCTAVIUS CAESAR and his train","id":"cleopatra:5.2:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He words me, girls, he words me, that I should not\nBe noble to myself: but, hark thee, Charmian.","sourceLine":"5.2.225","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:88"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Whispers CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Finish, good lady; the bright day is done,\nAnd we are for the dark.","sourceLine":"5.2.227","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hie thee again:\nI have spoke already, and it is provided;\nGo put it to the haste.","sourceLine":"5.2.229","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I will.","sourceLine":"5.2.232","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:92"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is the queen?","sourceLine":"5.2.233","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Behold, sir.","sourceLine":"5.2.234","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:95"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:5.2:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dolabella!","sourceLine":"5.2.235","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, as thereto sworn by your command,\nWhich my love makes religion to obey,\nI tell you this: Caesar through Syria\nIntends his journey; and within three days\nYou with your children will he send before:\nMake your best use of this: I have perform'd\nYour pleasure and my promise.","sourceLine":"5.2.236","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dolabella,\nI shall remain your debtor.","sourceLine":"5.2.243","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I your servant,\nAdieu, good queen; I must attend on Caesar.","sourceLine":"5.2.245","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, and thanks.","sourceLine":"5.2.247","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:101"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, Iras, what think'st thou?\nThou, an Egyptian puppet, shalt be shown\nIn Rome, as well as I mechanic slaves\nWith greasy aprons, rules, and hammers, shall\nUplift us to the view; in their thick breaths,\nRank of gross diet, shall be enclouded,\nAnd forced to drink their vapour.","sourceLine":"5.2.248","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods forbid!","sourceLine":"5.2.255","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, 'tis most certain, Iras: saucy lictors\nWill catch at us, like strumpets; and scald rhymers\nBallad us out o' tune: the quick comedians\nExtemporally will stage us, and present\nOur Alexandrian revels; Antony\nShall be brought drunken forth, and I shall see\nSome squeaking Cleopatra boy my greatness\nI' the posture of a whore.","sourceLine":"5.2.256","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O the good gods!","sourceLine":"5.2.264","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, that's certain.","sourceLine":"5.2.265","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll never see 't; for, I am sure, my nails\nAre stronger than mine eyes.","sourceLine":"5.2.266","speaker":"IRAS","id":"cleopatra:5.2:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, that's the way\nTo fool their preparation, and to conquer\nTheir most absurd intents.","sourceLine":"5.2.268","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:109"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, Charmian!\nShow me, my women, like a queen: go fetch\nMy best attires: I am again for Cydnus,\nTo meet Mark Antony: sirrah Iras, go.\nNow, noble Charmian, we'll dispatch indeed;\nAnd, when thou hast done this chare, I'll give thee leave\nTo play till doomsday. Bring our crown and all.\nWherefore's this noise?","sourceLine":"5.2.271","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:111"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit IRAS. A noise within","id":"cleopatra:5.2:112"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Guardsman","id":"cleopatra:5.2:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here is a rural fellow\nThat will not be denied your highness presence:\nHe brings you figs.","sourceLine":"5.2.279","speaker":"Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let him come in.","sourceLine":"5.2.282","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:115"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Guardsman","id":"cleopatra:5.2:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What poor an instrument\nMay do a noble deed! he brings me liberty.\nMy resolution's placed, and I have nothing\nOf woman in me: now from head to foot\nI am marble-constant; now the fleeting moon\nNo planet is of mine.","sourceLine":"5.2.283","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:117"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter Guardsman, with Clown bringing in a basket","id":"cleopatra:5.2:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is the man.","sourceLine":"5.2.289","speaker":"Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Avoid, and leave him.","sourceLine":"5.2.290","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:120"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Guardsman","id":"cleopatra:5.2:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hast thou the pretty worm of Nilus there,\nThat kills and pains not?","sourceLine":"5.2.291","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Truly, I have him: but I would not be the party\nthat should desire you to touch him, for his biting\nis immortal; those that do die of it do seldom or\nnever recover.","sourceLine":"5.2.293","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:123"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Rememberest thou any that have died on't?","sourceLine":"5.2.297","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:124"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Very many, men and women too. I heard of one of\nthem no longer than yesterday: a very honest woman,\nbut something given to lie; as a woman should not\ndo, but in the way of honesty: how she died of the\nbiting of it, what pain she felt: truly, she makes\na very good report o' the worm; but he that will\nbelieve all that they say, shall never be saved by\nhalf that they do: but this is most fallible, the\nworm's an odd worm.","sourceLine":"5.2.298","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:125"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Get thee hence; farewell.","sourceLine":"5.2.307","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:126"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I wish you all joy of the worm.","sourceLine":"5.2.308","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:127"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Setting down his basket","id":"cleopatra:5.2:128"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell.","sourceLine":"5.2.309","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:129"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You must think this, look you, that the worm will\ndo his kind.","sourceLine":"5.2.310","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:130"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, ay; farewell.","sourceLine":"5.2.312","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:131"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look you, the worm is not to be trusted but in the\nkeeping of wise people; for, indeed, there is no\ngoodness in worm.","sourceLine":"5.2.313","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:132"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take thou no care; it shall be heeded.","sourceLine":"5.2.316","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:133"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Very good. Give it nothing, I pray you, for it is\nnot worth the feeding.","sourceLine":"5.2.317","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:134"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will it eat me?","sourceLine":"5.2.319","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:135"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You must not think I am so simple but I know the\ndevil himself will not eat a woman: I know that a\nwoman is a dish for the gods, if the devil dress her\nnot. But, truly, these same whoreson devils do the\ngods great harm in their women; for in every ten\nthat they make, the devils mar five.","sourceLine":"5.2.320","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:136"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, get thee gone; farewell.","sourceLine":"5.2.326","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:137"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, forsooth: I wish you joy o' the worm.","sourceLine":"5.2.327","speaker":"Clown","id":"cleopatra:5.2:138"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"cleopatra:5.2:139"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter IRAS with a robe, crown, & c","id":"cleopatra:5.2:140"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Give me my robe, put on my crown; I have\nImmortal longings in me: now no more\nThe juice of Egypt's grape shall moist this lip:\nYare, yare, good Iras; quick. Methinks I hear\nAntony call; I see him rouse himself\nTo praise my noble act; I hear him mock\nThe luck of Caesar, which the gods give men\nTo excuse their after wrath: husband, I come:\nNow to that name my courage prove my title!\nI am fire and air; my other elements\nI give to baser life. So; have you done?\nCome then, and take the last warmth of my lips.\nFarewell, kind Charmian; Iras, long farewell.","sourceLine":"5.2.328","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:141"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Kisses them. IRAS falls and dies","id":"cleopatra:5.2:142"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have I the aspic in my lips? Dost fall?\nIf thou and nature can so gently part,\nThe stroke of death is as a lover's pinch,\nWhich hurts, and is desired. Dost thou lie still?\nIf thus thou vanishest, thou tell'st the world\nIt is not worth leave-taking.","sourceLine":"5.2.341","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:143"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dissolve, thick cloud, and rain; that I may say,\nThe gods themselves do weep!","sourceLine":"5.2.347","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:144"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This proves me base:\nIf she first meet the curled Antony,\nHe'll make demand of her, and spend that kiss\nWhich is my heaven to have. Come, thou\nmortal wretch,","sourceLine":"5.2.349","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:145"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To an asp, which she applies to her breast","id":"cleopatra:5.2:146"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With thy sharp teeth this knot intrinsicate\nOf life at once untie: poor venomous fool\nBe angry, and dispatch. O, couldst thou speak,\nThat I might hear thee call great Caesar ass\nUnpolicied!","sourceLine":"5.2.354","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:147"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O eastern star!","sourceLine":"5.2.359","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:148"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peace, peace!\nDost thou not see my baby at my breast,\nThat sucks the nurse asleep?","sourceLine":"5.2.360","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:149"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, break! O, break!","sourceLine":"5.2.363","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:150"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As sweet as balm, as soft as air, as gentle,--\nO Antony!--Nay, I will take thee too.","sourceLine":"5.2.364","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:151"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Applying another asp to her arm","id":"cleopatra:5.2:152"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What should I stay--","sourceLine":"5.2.366","speaker":"CLEOPATRA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:153"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Dies","id":"cleopatra:5.2:154"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In this vile world? So, fare thee well.\nNow boast thee, death, in thy possession lies\nA lass unparallel'd. Downy windows, close;\nAnd golden Phoebus never be beheld\nOf eyes again so royal! Your crown's awry;\nI'll mend it, and then play.","sourceLine":"5.2.367","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:155"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the Guard, rushing in","id":"cleopatra:5.2:156"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where is the queen?","sourceLine":"5.2.373","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:157"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Speak softly, wake her not.","sourceLine":"5.2.374","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:158"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar hath sent--","sourceLine":"5.2.375","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:159"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Too slow a messenger.","sourceLine":"5.2.376","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:160"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Applies an asp","id":"cleopatra:5.2:161"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, come apace, dispatch! I partly feel thee.","sourceLine":"5.2.377","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:162"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Approach, ho! All's not well: Caesar's beguiled.","sourceLine":"5.2.378","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:163"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's Dolabella sent from Caesar; call him.","sourceLine":"5.2.379","speaker":"Second Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:164"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What work is here! Charmian, is this well done?","sourceLine":"5.2.380","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:165"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is well done, and fitting for a princess\nDescended of so many royal kings.\nAh, soldier!","sourceLine":"5.2.381","speaker":"CHARMIAN","id":"cleopatra:5.2:166"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Dies","id":"cleopatra:5.2:167"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:168"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How goes it here?","sourceLine":"5.2.384","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:169"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All dead.","sourceLine":"5.2.385","speaker":"Second Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:170"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Caesar, thy thoughts\nTouch their effects in this: thyself art coming\nTo see perform'd the dreaded act which thou\nSo sought'st to hinder.","sourceLine":"5.2.386","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:171"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Within 'A way there, a way for Caesar!'","id":"cleopatra:5.2:172"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter OCTAVIUS CAESAR and all his train marching","id":"cleopatra:5.2:173"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O sir, you are too sure an augurer;\nThat you did fear is done.","sourceLine":"5.2.390","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:174"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bravest at the last,\nShe levell'd at our purposes, and, being royal,\nTook her own way. The manner of their deaths?\nI do not see them bleed.","sourceLine":"5.2.392","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:175"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who was last with them?","sourceLine":"5.2.396","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:176"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A simple countryman, that brought her figs:\nThis was his basket.","sourceLine":"5.2.397","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:177"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poison'd, then.","sourceLine":"5.2.399","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:178"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Caesar,\nThis Charmian lived but now; she stood and spake:\nI found her trimming up the diadem\nOn her dead mistress; tremblingly she stood\nAnd on the sudden dropp'd.","sourceLine":"5.2.400","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:179"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O noble weakness!\nIf they had swallow'd poison, 'twould appear\nBy external swelling: but she looks like sleep,\nAs she would catch another Antony\nIn her strong toil of grace.","sourceLine":"5.2.405","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:180"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, on her breast,\nThere is a vent of blood and something blown:\nThe like is on her arm.","sourceLine":"5.2.410","speaker":"DOLABELLA","id":"cleopatra:5.2:181"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is an aspic's trail: and these fig-leaves\nHave slime upon them, such as the aspic leaves\nUpon the caves of Nile.","sourceLine":"5.2.413","speaker":"First Guard","id":"cleopatra:5.2:182"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most probable\nThat so she died; for her physician tells me\nShe hath pursued conclusions infinite\nOf easy ways to die. Take up her bed;\nAnd bear her women from the monument:\nShe shall be buried by her Antony:\nNo grave upon the earth shall clip in it\nA pair so famous. High events as these\nStrike those that make them; and their story is\nNo less in pity than his glory which\nBrought them to be lamented. Our army shall\nIn solemn show attend this funeral;\nAnd then to Rome. Come, Dolabella, see\nHigh order in this great solemnity.","sourceLine":"5.2.416","speaker":"OCTAVIUS CAESAR","id":"cleopatra:5.2:183"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"cleopatra:5.2:184"}],"scene":2}],"sourceLineCount":3560,"sourceLabel":"MIT Shakespeare","sectionCount":42,"blockCount":1487,"wordCount":24852};
